gradient is a vector that

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:33.930
looks like entrywise
1 over x, right,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.680
and the Hessian is diag of like
1 over xi squared or something

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.690
like that.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.570
So the problem is
that those make sense

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.430
when the xI's are negative.

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.210
Those expressions, right,
they actually they parse.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:49.710
I mean, if you're unlucky enough
to have one of the xI zero,

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.970
then both of those are going
to give you some trouble.

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:53.530
But they're going to make sense.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:57.090
They are completely wrong and
have no meaning whatsoever

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.800
in this problem, right.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.480
Because for us, the function
is defined on x positive.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.230
Period.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.530
Right.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:08.200
So what that means is
in your line search,

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.960
you're going to want
to first make sure

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.650
that x plus TV is positive.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.370
And you keep reducing or
you have T times equals beta

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:20.410
until that becomes true.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:22.000
Then, you check the residual.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.430
Unfortunately, if you go
ahead and check the residual,

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.370
it's very likely to actually
make sense and everything

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:28.090
will be wrong.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:29.947
Nothing good is going
to happen after that.

00:01:29.947 --> 00:01:31.280
And it's going to be a disaster.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.260
So OK.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.530
