what its derivatives are.

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:44.340
So the derivatives
are basically,

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:46.500
it's the sum of the
gradients of the functions

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.180
and scaled, right?

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:49.800
fI of x is negative, right.

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:52.530
So that's-- this thing
you should interpret this

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:54.990
as the margin, right?

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:57.420
And the Hessian has
two terms, right?

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.310
It's got the second.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.160
It's got the Hessian of the
individual functions, right,

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.160
scaled by minus fI, which
is 1 over the margin, which

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:06.240
is non-negative, right.

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:08.880
And then it's got a
bunch of rank 1 terms

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:10.260
which has to do
with the gradient

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:14.310
and that's scaled by 1 over
fI of x squared, right,

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:18.030
which is basically the margin,
the square of the margin, OK.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:23.160
So these are what these
two things look like.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:27.000
And again, all sorts
of stuff should

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.120
be storing in your mind
for possible exploitation

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:33.210
like diagonal plus
low rank or something.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.340
OK.

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:39.360
So what we do is we're going
to multiply through the t.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:41.430
It had been 1 over t times phi.

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:43.140
We just put the t over nf zero.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.450
It's the same-- it's
equivalent problem, right.

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:47.490
And now, what we're
going to assume,

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:50.160
we're going to assume that
problem up there has a solution

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.590
for each positive t, right.

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:55.500
So we'll just assume that.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:59.040
And then what that means is we
can actually parameterize it

00:12:59.040 --> 00:12:59.580
by t.

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:00.955
And so you get
something really--

00:13:00.955 --> 00:13:02.850
you get this thing which
is like x star of t.

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:05.160
It's the solution of this
problem as a function

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.110
of this parameter t.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:09.060
So that's what it is, right?

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:14.310
So when t is--

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:15.340
well, let's see.

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:15.840
OK.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:16.680
Yeah.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:18.030
Well, we can go through this.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:20.790
As t goes to
infinity, we imagine

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:24.030
this to be a really
good approximation

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:27.480
of the real function,
which is like f 0

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:32.010
plus then the indicator function
of all of the constraints,

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:33.090
right.

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:36.580
So this gives a curve in rn
parameterized by this parameter

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:37.080
t.

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:37.710
This thing.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:41.170
And this is called the
central path this curve.

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:43.870
So that's the name of it.

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:46.590
And here's just a baby picture
to show you what it looks like.

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:49.180
So here's a polyhedron here.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:50.640
So the original
problem was an LP.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:54.340
These are the constraints.

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:57.000
Let's see what
happens when I set

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:01.170
t equals very small,
like zero, I'm

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:03.593
minimizing that log barrier
over this polyhedron.

00:14:03.593 --> 00:14:05.010
And that's going
to give me-- it's

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:06.510
going to start at
the analytic center.

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:08.885
So that's going to be the
analytic center of this, right.

00:14:08.885 --> 00:14:11.400
So and in fact, if you'll
recall at this point,

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:15.305
maximizes the product
of the margins here,

00:14:15.305 --> 00:14:16.930
which is the same as
maximizing the sum

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:18.320
of the logs of the margins.

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:18.820
OK.

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:20.690
Now, as t gets
bigger and bigger,

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:24.100
I'm bringing in tc
transpose x because that's

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:26.020
the objective here.

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:29.530
And what happens is
it moves along here

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:31.420
and you can actually
work out exactly

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:33.700
what happens when I choose.

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:35.720
This is of 10.

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:39.460
So what's going to happen is
this is the optimal point.

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:40.910
c points this way.

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:43.000
So the whole LP says,
please, go as far as you

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:45.550
can this direction
in this polyhedron,

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:47.050
and that's the
optimal point, right.

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:50.410
And so here is the
so-called central path.

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:52.210
And you can actually
see what happens.

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:53.200
This is when it's 0.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:54.492
This is when it's 10.

00:14:54.492 --> 00:14:56.200
I don't know what--
this is maybe 20, 30.

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:59.830
But as you increase
t, this is indeed--

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:01.253
you know, it's a 2D example.

00:15:01.253 --> 00:15:02.170
So it's kind of silly.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:07.468
But it is indeed moving
towards an optimal--

00:15:07.468 --> 00:15:09.760
well, in this case, I can
say the optimal point, right.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:12.070
So that's-- so that all
kind of makes sense.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:14.780
That's the idea.

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:15.280
OK.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.320
So this is the picture.

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:19.840
Now of course, you have to
visualize this in a thousand

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:22.030
dimensions really to
understand how all this works

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:23.530
and stuff like that.

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:25.970
But this is a good enough
picture to get the idea.

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:26.470
OK.

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:31.770
Something very cool happens.

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:33.420
And that's this.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:36.540
If you are on the
central path, then it

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:40.080
means that you solve
this problem right here.

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:41.710
You solve this problem.

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:43.380
Well, the KKT
conditions for that

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:45.375
are going to be, Ax equals b.

00:15:45.375 --> 00:15:46.930
That's primal feasibility.

00:15:46.930 --> 00:15:53.250
And then t grad f of 0 plus
grad phi plus a transpose nu.

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:55.170
And that's going to
be the equals zero.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:59.170
That's going to be the dual
part of the KKT conditions.

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:01.780
And so these are the
optimality conditions.

00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:06.990
And here, I've simply
expanded out the--

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:08.340
let's see.

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:13.890
I have expanded out the
gradient of the barrier.

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:15.780
And it looks like this
thing here, right?

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:20.020
So it looks like that.

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:21.180
So OK.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:23.640
That's fine.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:27.780
But if you stare at
this very, very closely,

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:31.950
if I tell you that this is 0
and I look at the coefficients

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:33.240
here, I divide by t.

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:35.640
So that's grad of 0.

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:38.340
This is 1 over minus t fI of x.

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:41.590
That's a positive number
because fI of x is less than 0.

00:16:41.590 --> 00:16:42.090
Right?

00:16:42.090 --> 00:16:44.040
That's required here.

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:46.380
And I see that
hey, look at that.

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:51.030
When Ix star of t satisfies
the gradient of f0

00:16:51.030 --> 00:16:54.570
plus a sum of
positive numbers times

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:58.403
the gradients of fI
plus a transpose w is 0.

00:16:58.403 --> 00:17:00.570
And then you think about
that and you think about it

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:02.580
in your head and
you say, oh my god.

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:05.490
That means that
this thing actually

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:10.290
minimizes the Lagrangian but
for these specific choices.

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:12.510
So nu is w over t.

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:17.640
And the lambda I's are 1
over t times the margins.

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:20.339
Make sense?

00:17:20.339 --> 00:17:21.920
OK.

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:23.510
Well, this is cool.

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:27.050
Because if you have a
dual feasible-- if you

00:17:27.050 --> 00:17:30.350
have a point that minimizes the
Lagrangian, that means you just

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:32.730
evaluated g, the dual function.

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:34.700
And if you evaluate
the dual function

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:39.710
at this particular
lambda and this nu here,

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:40.970
you just work out what it is.

00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:42.510
It's just the
Lagrangian of this.

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:45.800
And when I plug-in what
it is at lambda star here,

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:50.000
if I plug that in here,
I get something actually

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:50.960
kind of amazing.

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:54.650
Because you'll remember that--
so each of these is that.

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:57.980
So when I plug that in
there, the fI is just cancel.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:17:58.640
Period.

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:02.600
They cancel and you get like
basically minus 1 over t.

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:05.040
You add up m of those
and you get this term.

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:08.960
And so you get something
amazing that looks like this.

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:11.190
So let's actually read it.

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:12.570
It's super simple.

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:17.180
It says that this is
the current value--

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:19.070
x of t is strictly
feasible, right?

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:22.050
So this is sort of an upper
bound on the optimal value p

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:23.250
star right.

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:28.230
What this says is that if you
subtract m over t from it,

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:32.440
that number is less
than the optimal value.

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:35.850
Another way to say it is that
the gap between your value

00:18:35.850 --> 00:18:40.210
and p star is less
than m over t.

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:43.090
So that's actually-- so
let me-- let's go back

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:44.270
and see what that says.

00:18:44.270 --> 00:18:49.510
That says that if
you simply solve--

00:18:49.510 --> 00:18:51.370
let's see-- if you solve--

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:52.850
I'll find the-- here it is.

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:55.130
So if you simply solve
this problem, which

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:57.790
you know how to do because
you just use Newton's method,

00:18:57.790 --> 00:19:00.650
then two things are
going to happen.

00:19:00.650 --> 00:19:03.410
Number one, you will,
whether you like it or not,

00:19:03.410 --> 00:19:06.740
you're going to inadvertently
produce dual feasible points.

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:09.710
Those dual feasible points are
going to give you a bound on p

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:10.930
star.

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:13.810
Not only that, we
know exactly the gap.

00:19:13.810 --> 00:19:15.880
And the gap is defined
as the difference

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:20.710
between your current objective
value and your best mp star.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:22.930
It's exactly m over t.

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:24.670
And so that's
actually very cool.

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:27.730
It completely confirms all
this like hand-waving about

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:30.300
if t gets big, right,
I mean here would be--

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:31.600
my hand-waving is yeah, sure.

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:33.070
If t gets big,
that approximates.

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:35.140
That that's got to be
close to the real thing.

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:37.370
But this is just very simple.

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:41.320
It just says, when you compute
a point on the central path,

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:45.310
you will find actually a
primal point and a dual--

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:48.070
and a pair of dual
points and the gap

00:19:48.070 --> 00:19:49.420
is going to be m over t.

00:19:49.420 --> 00:19:52.820
So if t goes to infinity
now, you solve the problem.

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:55.000
And that's completely clear.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:56.110
Yeah.

00:19:56.110 --> 00:19:58.240
What is the difference
between doing it this way

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:01.180
and just starting by
taking the Lagrangian

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:03.260
of the original problem
and doing it from there?

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:03.760
Oh.

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:04.260
Yeah.

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:06.020
That's a great question.

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:10.210
So for many problems, LP would
be a perfect example, right?

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:13.252
If you just pick
random dual variables

00:20:13.252 --> 00:20:14.710
and you evaluate
the dual function,

00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:16.172
you get minus infinity, right?

00:20:16.172 --> 00:20:18.130
Because for example, for
an LP and for actually

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:20.500
for many problems,
the dual function

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:24.310
is minus infinity unless some
very specific things happen

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:25.960
like equality
constraints hold, right.

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:28.720
So if I say here's my
LP and you say, oh,

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:31.180
fine, I'm going to
form a Lagrangian,

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:34.302
then how do you pick your
dual variables, right?

00:20:34.302 --> 00:20:36.010
The answer is if you're
doing it randomly

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:39.100
or something, you're just
going to get, congratulations,

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:40.750
your lower bound
is minus infinity.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:43.280
This is very cool because
this says you first

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:47.360
you solve a smooth equality
constrained problem

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:49.760
and then that out
of that is going

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:51.920
to come some dual variables
that are guaranteed

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:52.940
to be dual feasible.

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:56.390
And you'll get-- not only will
you get a non-trivial not--

00:20:56.390 --> 00:20:58.532
in other words, not minus
infinity lower bound,

00:20:58.532 --> 00:21:00.740
you're going to get one
that's actually the value you

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:02.780
get minus m over t.

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:04.460
I don't know if that made sense.

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:08.390
So here, you are finding the
dual variables inadvertently

00:21:08.390 --> 00:21:11.970
by minimizing this
barrier function, right?

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:16.910
But there are problems
where if the domain of g

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:20.000
is everything where you can
just update the dual variables

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:20.970
and stuff like that.

00:21:20.970 --> 00:21:24.980
So actually, those are
often called price--

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:26.450
either price directed methods.

00:21:26.450 --> 00:21:29.570
That's-- in economics, that's
what they're referred to.

00:21:29.570 --> 00:21:31.010
But it doesn't work for most--

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:33.470
if you have an LP or something,
it doesn't work at all.

00:21:33.470 --> 00:21:35.000
Because those--
roughly speaking,

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:38.720
those prices have to satisfy
extremely specific properties.

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:40.580
So OK.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:41.540
Good.

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:43.290
OK.

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:47.430
Now, we can also interpret
this via KKT conditions, right?

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:51.400
So let's see how that works.

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:51.910
OK.

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:54.550
So if you look at
x star of t, that's

00:21:54.550 --> 00:21:56.995
the thing you compute
by Newton's method.

00:21:56.995 --> 00:21:58.870
Well, in fact, I think
the verb you would use

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:01.720
is you have centered--
you've actually centered

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:04.600
or you've computed a point
on the central path, right.

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:06.910
You've computed x of t here.

00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:09.430
And then from that, we get
these lambda stars, right.

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:12.790
These 1 over t times the margins
and blah, blah, blah, right?

00:22:12.790 --> 00:22:14.860
So I get those.

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:17.950
And they are also
functions of t.

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:18.850
I think-- yeah.

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:19.660
That's fine.

00:22:19.660 --> 00:22:20.740
So we do that.

00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:23.290
And now, this is
a primal dual pair

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:25.360
and let's see what they satisfy.

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:28.885
Well, not only do
they satisfy these,

00:22:28.885 --> 00:22:31.370
these are actually satisfied
with a strict inequality,

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:31.870
right.

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:33.490
Because you have
this log barrier so

00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:36.010
you can't even be zero.

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:38.410
So primal feasibility works.

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:40.725
The Ax equals b comes
for free because you've

00:22:40.725 --> 00:22:42.850
solved the problem with
the constraint Ax equals b.

00:22:42.850 --> 00:22:44.560
So that's free.

00:22:44.560 --> 00:22:46.540
Your dual constraints are--

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:48.607
well, these are definitely
bigger and equal to 0.

00:22:48.607 --> 00:22:50.190
As a matter of fact,
they're positive.

00:22:50.190 --> 00:22:51.773
If you look at what
those numbers are,

00:22:51.773 --> 00:22:54.620
they're 1 over the
margins times t.

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:56.450
And t is positive.

00:22:56.450 --> 00:22:58.320
And the margin is
positive and all that.

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:01.730
So in fact, this inequality
and this one hold with strict.

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:02.660
They're strict.

00:23:02.660 --> 00:23:07.070
But this is all we
really want here.

00:23:07.070 --> 00:23:08.810
If you go through
and work out-- well,

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:12.170
this is actually, by definition,
remember that we define lambda

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:16.970
I to be 1 over minus t fI of x.

00:23:16.970 --> 00:23:20.850
And so I express that this way.

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:24.320
And then the final part is
the gradient of the Lagrangian

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:25.340
is zero, right.

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:27.740
So that's fine.

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:28.250
OK.

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:30.193
Now, the only-- if you
stare at this and say,

00:23:30.193 --> 00:23:31.610
what's the difference
between this

00:23:31.610 --> 00:23:33.990
and the KKT conditions
of the original problem,

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:37.760
there's only one difference
and it's really small.

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:39.650
It's right here.

00:23:39.650 --> 00:23:41.930
This is the complementary
slackness condition.

00:23:41.930 --> 00:23:47.750
And in the real KKT conditions,
if lambda I fI were 0,

00:23:47.750 --> 00:23:49.730
then that would be--

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:52.130
these would literally be
the optimality conditions

00:23:52.130 --> 00:23:53.520
for the original problem.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:54.020
OK.

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:58.010
So what's happened is
you've simply replaced them

00:23:58.010 --> 00:24:03.230
with instead of 0, you have
1 over t. t is positive.

00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:06.060
And as a matter of fact, t is
going to get bigger and bigger.

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:08.690
So you're actually
saying, well, you're

00:24:08.690 --> 00:24:12.290
actually computing points that
satisfy all the KKT except one.

00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:15.590
And then it almost satisfies
it, especially when t is large.

00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:17.480
Everybody got that?

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:19.820
So in fact, I think
a lot of derivations

00:24:19.820 --> 00:24:21.380
of this start that way.

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:24.170
They say here's
the KKT conditions.

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:26.960
You put a 0 over there and
they go, OK, we're going

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:29.270
to replace 0 with 1 over t.

00:24:29.270 --> 00:24:31.760
And then you're like, well,
then how would you solve this,

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:32.260
right?

00:24:32.260 --> 00:24:33.440
But now you know, right.

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:36.080
You solve it by doing
this centering problem.

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:37.620
Everybody following this?

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:40.070
So it's a very good
beautiful interpretation.

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:45.930
There's also a really cool, like
you can imagine, a force field

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:46.980
interpretation.

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:49.507
So forget the equality
constraints just

00:24:49.507 --> 00:24:50.340
to make this simple.

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:54.120
But so we're going to minimize
this function here, right?

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:58.410
And what I do is I'll think of
a mechanical interpretation.

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:01.620
So I think of x as a
generalized position

00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:04.680
or configuration or something
light of some system, right.

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:10.860
And then what I do is I
have a-- so this thing

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:13.350
is going to be the potential
of some force field.

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:15.570
The force field is the
gradient-- negative gradient

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:16.920
of a potential, right.

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:21.420
So what I do is this is
my-- that's my force field.

00:25:21.420 --> 00:25:26.370
And then here, I have a--
that's the potential of a force

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:28.860
field associated
with each constraint.

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:34.320
And at this central
point, the force fields,

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:35.830
all the forces balance, right.

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:38.010
It just means the gradients
sum up to zero, right?

00:25:38.010 --> 00:25:39.090
So that's the picture.

00:25:39.090 --> 00:25:40.890
And let me show you--
what we'll show you--

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:44.240
I'll just show you some pictures
about how you can look at that.

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:46.980
So here's an LP.

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:50.880
And the way we're going
to solve it is this.

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:56.850
We'll introduce a force field
which is just minus t times c.

00:25:56.850 --> 00:26:00.300
So it just means it's
a constant force field.

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:01.980
It's like gravity
except it doesn't

00:26:01.980 --> 00:26:04.140
happen to be pointing
straight down, right?

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:05.970
It's pointing-- actually, it's--

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:08.910
the force is pointing actually
straight in the negative c

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:11.730
direction and it's
constant multiplied

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:13.320
by know if you fix t.

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:16.680
Everybody got-- so that's
just a constant force field.

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:20.670
Then, for each of
these constraints here,

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:23.250
what we have is a
repulsive force.

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:25.800
In other words, if
you're here, it's

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:28.695
always pointing directly
away from these things.

00:26:28.695 --> 00:26:29.820
In fact, that's what it is.

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:36.360
It points directly away and
it's inversely proportional to,

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:37.950
I could say, the
distance, right,

00:26:37.950 --> 00:26:39.750
to the distance or
the margin, right?

00:26:39.750 --> 00:26:42.390
So what that says is associated
with this constraint.

00:26:42.390 --> 00:26:45.570
This constraint says
don't be over here, right.

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:48.550
And what the contribution
to the force field

00:26:48.550 --> 00:26:50.420
is as a force field,
if I'm right here,

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:52.520
it's extremely strong
and pointing this way.

00:26:52.520 --> 00:26:54.700
If I'm way over
here, it's very mild.

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:57.550
But it is pointing
directly away from here.

00:26:57.550 --> 00:27:00.880
So as you move closer here,
you get a much stronger force

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:02.050
field.

00:27:02.050 --> 00:27:03.760
I mean, this is only--

00:27:03.760 --> 00:27:06.870
if this helps you
visualize this, good.

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:09.400
If it doesn't, that's fine too.

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:12.370
But just to get the picture.

00:27:12.370 --> 00:27:17.440
So here at this point,
that's t equals 1,

00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:19.330
we have, I guess
in this case, we

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:21.800
have-- let's see-- one, two,
three, four, five constraints.

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:23.950
So these are the
forces associated

00:27:23.950 --> 00:27:24.830
with the constraints.

00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:26.110
This is minus c.

00:27:26.110 --> 00:27:28.390
So I have a constant force
which is-- oh, by the way,

00:27:28.390 --> 00:27:31.390
the objective force is
constant everywhere in here.

00:27:31.390 --> 00:27:34.910
It just says please go
in this direction, right.

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:36.550
I mean, I can multiply it by--

00:27:36.550 --> 00:27:39.910
I'll multiply it by t which will
scale up the effect of that.

00:27:39.910 --> 00:27:41.020
So OK.

00:27:41.020 --> 00:27:45.010
And here, so this here
is the repulsive force

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:47.520
from this constraint.

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:53.080
This is the repulsive force from
this constraint here, right?

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:57.580
This is the repulsive force
from this constraint, right?

00:27:57.580 --> 00:27:58.690
And then, I don't know.

00:27:58.690 --> 00:27:59.607
You could work it out.

00:27:59.607 --> 00:28:01.150
I mean, it's true.

00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:03.980
All of these forces balance at
this point on the central path,

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:04.480
right.

00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:06.080
And so now you can
see all sorts of cool.

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:07.497
You can actually
kind of visualize

00:28:07.497 --> 00:28:09.340
what happens when I increase t.

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:14.410
When I increase t,
this force here, it--

00:28:14.410 --> 00:28:15.580
I multiplied it by three.

00:28:15.580 --> 00:28:18.770
It gets three times stronger,
pushing you in this direction.

00:28:18.770 --> 00:28:22.200
And so it settles to a new
equilibrium, which is there.

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:24.700
I mean, you can see it along
the central path and everything

00:28:24.700 --> 00:28:25.990
makes sense.

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:27.197
You move this way.

00:28:27.197 --> 00:28:28.780
And as you did, you
got closer to this

00:28:28.780 --> 00:28:30.220
and then this got bigger, right.

00:28:30.220 --> 00:28:32.350
You got closer to this
one, this got bigger.

00:28:32.350 --> 00:28:34.780
And whatever it
is, these things,

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:37.990
these six force vectors
are going to add to zero.

00:28:37.990 --> 00:28:40.630
So I don't know if this is
helpful or not for people.

00:28:40.630 --> 00:28:44.930
But for me, I think it's a
very good way to see this.

00:28:44.930 --> 00:28:45.430
OK.

00:28:45.430 --> 00:28:49.360
Let me ask a question about it.

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:54.030
What if I take a poly-- what
if I add a redundant constraint

00:28:54.030 --> 00:28:55.610
to an LP?

00:28:55.610 --> 00:29:02.060
So I add a new A, vector
A, a transpose x less than

00:29:02.060 --> 00:29:03.860
or equal to b and is redundant.

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:08.155
Meaning that visually, it
means it's like it's over here.

00:29:08.155 --> 00:29:09.530
It says please
stay on that side.

00:29:09.530 --> 00:29:11.400
But everything already in
here is on that side of it.

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:12.480
Everybody got that?

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:14.570
So here's my
question for you does

00:29:14.570 --> 00:29:15.695
it change the central path?

00:29:27.507 --> 00:29:28.590
Here's another way to say.

00:29:28.590 --> 00:29:33.220
Do redundant inequalities have
an effect on the central path?

00:29:33.220 --> 00:29:35.030
That's my question.

00:29:35.030 --> 00:29:35.870
What do you think?

00:29:42.030 --> 00:29:42.970
No.

00:29:42.970 --> 00:29:43.470
OK.

00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:45.150
What's your argument?

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:46.380
They're are redundant.

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:48.300
They're redundant.

00:29:48.300 --> 00:29:49.630
I like that argument.

00:29:49.630 --> 00:29:50.130
OK.

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:51.445
So here's what--

00:29:51.445 --> 00:29:52.320
I like your argument.

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:54.370
OK it's got-- it's brief.

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:57.180
So that's always a plus.

00:29:57.180 --> 00:29:58.530
Let's see.

00:29:58.530 --> 00:29:59.400
OK.

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:01.650
It does have one disadvantage.

00:30:01.650 --> 00:30:02.820
Also brief.

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:05.280
It's false, right?

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:08.770
But it sounded-- it
sounds good and it's good.

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:09.270
OK.

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:10.518
So look.

00:30:10.518 --> 00:30:11.560
Here's what would happen.

00:30:11.560 --> 00:30:14.070
I mean, let's even think
about what would happen.

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:17.640
What if I shoved an
inequality here and here

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:19.380
and I put a whole
bunch over here, right?

00:30:19.380 --> 00:30:20.940
Each of those is
producing a force.

00:30:20.940 --> 00:30:22.770
Now, they're not
inside here to--

00:30:22.770 --> 00:30:24.990
but it's going to feel this.

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:28.940
If suppose I put like 10
constraints right here,

00:30:28.940 --> 00:30:31.240
someone tell me what do you
think the central path is

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:32.240
going to look like then?

00:30:34.725 --> 00:30:35.600
Curve more downwards.

00:30:35.600 --> 00:30:36.387
Precisely.

00:30:36.387 --> 00:30:38.720
But it's going to still sneak
up to the solution, right?

00:30:38.720 --> 00:30:41.177
Because there's all these
repulsive forces this way,

00:30:41.177 --> 00:30:43.010
so it's going to actually
do something weird

00:30:43.010 --> 00:30:44.367
like go along this way, right.

00:30:44.367 --> 00:30:45.950
But the same story
is going to happen.

00:30:45.950 --> 00:30:49.200
It's going to come-- it's going
to converge to the solution.

00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:49.700
OK.

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:52.890
So again, this is just
for interpretation

00:30:52.890 --> 00:30:56.000
so you understand how
these things work.

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:58.640
Actually, when we get to
actually see how they work,

00:30:58.640 --> 00:31:00.670
no one understands
how they work.

00:31:00.670 --> 00:31:04.290
But if you want to pretend
to understand how they work,

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:07.070
this is a good picture
to do that with, right.

00:31:07.070 --> 00:31:09.580
So OK.

00:31:09.580 --> 00:31:10.080
OK.

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:11.940
So this brings us to
the barrier method.

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:15.640
Actually, before I-- yeah.

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:17.410
Actually before I
do this, I'm going

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:20.170
to talk about another
method before that.

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:21.460
And it's historical.

00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:25.570
So by the way, some of the
history is that a lot of this

00:31:25.570 --> 00:31:29.170
was worked out
actually in the 1960s.

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:31.270
It had names like
barrier method.

00:31:31.270 --> 00:31:34.000
And I had-- actually, the famous
method is something called

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:37.990
SUMT, S-U-M-T, is Sequential
Unconstrained Minimization

00:31:37.990 --> 00:31:39.040
Technique.

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:40.780
That's from the '60s.

00:31:40.780 --> 00:31:42.880
It was discovered
that someone named

00:31:42.880 --> 00:31:45.520
Deakin in the Soviet
Union had worked out

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:50.310
actually a completely modern
algorithm for all this in 1967.

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:56.180
And I guess my friends in
Moscow said it was hardly known

00:31:56.180 --> 00:31:59.720
or he said it was ignored
by people in Moscow.

00:31:59.720 --> 00:32:02.660
He said an unknown in the West.

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:05.570
That was his-- anyway,
it was actually

00:32:05.570 --> 00:32:07.767
everything that
two decades later

00:32:07.767 --> 00:32:09.350
was going to be--
was going to make it

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:12.980
to the front page of The New
York Times was in this 1967

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:18.210
paper by some guy in some hick
province or way off somewhere.

00:32:18.210 --> 00:32:19.710
I don't where it was.

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:20.210
OK.

00:32:20.210 --> 00:32:21.770
So all right.

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:26.600
So then in the US, there were
people, Fiacco and McCormick,

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:29.088
had come up with these
things called the Sequential

00:32:29.088 --> 00:32:30.630
Unconstrained
Optimization Technique.

00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:32.490
So let me explain what it is.

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:34.030
Let's go back.

00:32:34.030 --> 00:32:37.060
And well, actually, I guess
you already know all the story.

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:39.010
But it goes like this.

00:32:39.010 --> 00:32:41.560
You want to-- now that we know--

00:32:41.560 --> 00:32:44.167
when you-- you know how to
solve that problem up there.

00:32:44.167 --> 00:32:45.250
It's just Newton's method.

00:32:45.250 --> 00:32:46.990
You know how to do it.

00:32:46.990 --> 00:32:48.580
When you do it,
actually, not only

00:32:48.580 --> 00:32:51.160
will you-- when you
solve that problem,

00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:53.320
you'll get an
approximate solution

00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:54.670
of the original problem.

00:32:54.670 --> 00:32:56.290
But with a very
strong approximation,

00:32:56.290 --> 00:33:00.190
you're going to get both a
primal and a dual solution

00:33:00.190 --> 00:33:02.590
candidates and
the associated gap

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:05.380
is going to be exactly m over t.

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:09.190
So in some sense,
you're just done.

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:10.360
Like that's done.

00:33:10.360 --> 00:33:12.580
You just say, did you
want to solve an LP?

00:33:12.580 --> 00:33:15.220
Then I'd say, to what accuracy
do you want to solve it?

00:33:15.220 --> 00:33:17.200
And someone says 1e minus 5.

00:33:17.200 --> 00:33:18.040
And I go, cool.

00:33:18.040 --> 00:33:19.330
Then what's your m?

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:22.440
And now I choose t.

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:24.370
And it's an approximation
that comes back

00:33:24.370 --> 00:33:27.960
with a certificate
certifying its accuracy.

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:29.620
Everybody got this?

00:33:29.620 --> 00:33:31.370
And that doesn't have a name.

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:35.410
But I would call it UMT for
Unconstrained Minimization

00:33:35.410 --> 00:33:36.130
Technique.

00:33:36.130 --> 00:33:39.730
And it's just-- all it
means is you approximated--

00:33:39.730 --> 00:33:42.700
I mean it's basically back to
that story originally where we

00:33:42.700 --> 00:33:43.270
just--

00:33:43.270 --> 00:33:45.940
we approximated this
indicator function here

00:33:45.940 --> 00:33:48.800
with something smooth and then
we applied Newton's method.

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:51.710
But the nice part is we
got a lot of cool stuff

00:33:51.710 --> 00:33:54.242
there where we know if you
use log barrier and all

00:33:54.242 --> 00:33:55.700
that kind of stuff,
you're actually

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:57.938
going to get dual feasible
points and you get a gap

00:33:57.938 --> 00:33:59.480
and you know exactly
what the gap is.

00:33:59.480 --> 00:34:00.950
Everybody see this?

00:34:00.950 --> 00:34:03.080
So actually,
hilariously, that method

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:07.190
is perfectly good for a tons
of practical problems, right?

00:34:07.190 --> 00:34:08.900
So just so you know.

00:34:08.900 --> 00:34:10.580
Of course, it's not generic.

00:34:10.580 --> 00:34:12.800
You have to choose
the t very carefully.

00:34:12.800 --> 00:34:14.810
And everything you
think about it is right.

00:34:14.810 --> 00:34:16.489
That if you choose t too large--

00:34:16.489 --> 00:34:18.889
the larger t is, the better
approximation you get.

00:34:18.889 --> 00:34:22.074
Smaller-- but also the
larger the number of Newton

00:34:22.074 --> 00:34:23.449
steps it's going
to take to solve

00:34:23.449 --> 00:34:24.420
it and all that kind of stuff.

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:25.212
Everybody got that?

00:34:25.212 --> 00:34:27.170
So that's UMT.

00:34:27.170 --> 00:34:29.429
OK.

00:34:29.429 --> 00:34:29.929
All right.

00:34:29.929 --> 00:34:32.659
So if you try-- if you ask
for high accuracy or even

00:34:32.659 --> 00:34:36.590
modest accuracy on
something like an LP,

00:34:36.590 --> 00:34:38.870
then the problem is
it's going to take

00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:40.909
a couple of hundred
Newton steps or thousands

00:34:40.909 --> 00:34:41.940
or something like that.

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:43.850
And not only that, probably
with numerical errors,

00:34:43.850 --> 00:34:45.380
it's probably not even
going to converge, right?

00:34:45.380 --> 00:34:47.547
But if you did it with
infinite precision, it would.

00:34:47.547 --> 00:34:49.540
Everybody following this?

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:51.050
OK.

00:34:51.050 --> 00:34:51.550
OK.

00:34:51.550 --> 00:34:55.449
So then I have to tell you
about something else, which

00:34:55.449 --> 00:34:56.199
is the background.

00:34:56.199 --> 00:34:58.090
There's a whole
class of problems

00:34:58.090 --> 00:35:02.140
in applied math and computation
called homotopy methods.

00:35:02.140 --> 00:35:04.210
I don't know if people
heard about this before.

00:35:04.210 --> 00:35:06.210
Somewhere.

00:35:06.210 --> 00:35:06.990
OK.

00:35:06.990 --> 00:35:08.135
This is an awesome thing.

00:35:08.135 --> 00:35:09.510
Everybody should
know about this.

00:35:09.510 --> 00:35:11.860
It's very, very cool idea.

00:35:11.860 --> 00:35:12.778
It's this.

00:35:12.778 --> 00:35:14.320
You have a problem
you want to solve,

00:35:14.320 --> 00:35:16.300
which is either like an
optimization problem.

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:18.008
But typically, it's
actually just solving

00:35:18.008 --> 00:35:19.230
a set of nonlinear equations.

00:35:19.230 --> 00:35:21.390
You just want to-- so
here's an example, right?

00:35:21.390 --> 00:35:23.400
You're doing a spice simulation.

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:24.482
And you want to know--

00:35:24.482 --> 00:35:26.940
you want to find an equilibrium
point of the circuit, which

00:35:26.940 --> 00:35:29.320
has got some the DC-- whatever.

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:29.820
I'm sorry.

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:30.870
I'm speaking dialect now.

00:35:30.870 --> 00:35:33.180
But that's what you want to do.

00:35:33.180 --> 00:35:36.030
So that's a perfect
example of this.

00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:38.610
But it comes up in
tons of other stuff.

00:35:38.610 --> 00:35:39.660
So just OK.

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:41.650
So here's how it works.

00:35:41.650 --> 00:35:45.480
You have your problem and
you introduce a parameter.

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:47.790
They usually call it
maybe theta or something.

00:35:47.790 --> 00:35:50.520
But they have different kappas
and other train of-- anyway.

00:35:50.520 --> 00:35:52.050
But here it's t.

00:35:52.050 --> 00:35:53.610
You introduce a parameter.

00:35:53.610 --> 00:35:55.200
In fact in the
classical version,

00:35:55.200 --> 00:35:58.500
you introduce a parameter
with the following thing

00:35:58.500 --> 00:36:03.420
that perturbs, or
simplifies, or smooths out

00:36:03.420 --> 00:36:04.710
your original problem.

00:36:04.710 --> 00:36:09.670
And when you turn that knob,
the traditional range is 0 to 1.

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:11.170
I'll still use the--

00:36:11.170 --> 00:36:12.730
I'll use the parameter t, right.

00:36:12.730 --> 00:36:14.140
When t equals 0--

00:36:14.140 --> 00:36:16.390
when t equals 1, you get
the problem you really

00:36:16.390 --> 00:36:18.160
want to solve, right?

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:19.890
That's the one you
really want to solve.

00:36:19.890 --> 00:36:22.440
When t equals 0,
you get a problem

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:25.590
that you know the solution to.

00:36:25.590 --> 00:36:27.810
Everybody following this?

00:36:27.810 --> 00:36:29.200
Now, you do the following.

00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:29.700
Oh.

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:33.090
And then what you
do is you attempt

00:36:33.090 --> 00:36:35.010
to solve the problem
for t's and you're

00:36:35.010 --> 00:36:36.780
going to march along this path.

00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:39.150
And by the way, the
subtitle of a book on this

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:40.830
is called-- there's
a great subtitle.

00:36:40.830 --> 00:36:42.480
It's called Homotopy Methods.

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:46.890
And the subtitle is pathways to
solutions which is like, wow,

00:36:46.890 --> 00:36:48.510
that's like almost poetic.

00:36:48.510 --> 00:36:50.820
That's what it is, right?

00:36:50.820 --> 00:36:54.960
So the idea of a homotopy method
is you start with t equals 0.

00:36:54.960 --> 00:36:56.760
You know the solution
already, right?

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:58.710
Then you crank t up
a little bit and you

00:36:58.710 --> 00:37:00.490
attempt to solve the problem.

00:37:00.490 --> 00:37:04.230
And if you solve it-- this is
in the general case, right?

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:05.700
If you solve it, good for you.

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:08.077
Then you crank up t
again and you keep going.

00:37:08.077 --> 00:37:10.410
And when you get to t equals
1, you solved your problem.

00:37:10.410 --> 00:37:10.910
Oh.

00:37:10.910 --> 00:37:12.420
And what you would
typically do is

00:37:12.420 --> 00:37:14.490
let's say you're using
Newton's method each time,

00:37:14.490 --> 00:37:17.800
each time you would
start Newton's method

00:37:17.800 --> 00:37:19.360
from where you were before.

00:37:19.360 --> 00:37:22.820
That that's the key.

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:24.200
Everybody got this, right?

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:26.870
So matter of fact,
this is exactly

00:37:26.870 --> 00:37:29.180
how spice for those
of you who-- actually,

00:37:29.180 --> 00:37:31.850
how many people know about spice
or have used it or something?

00:37:31.850 --> 00:37:32.460
OK, so fine.

00:37:32.460 --> 00:37:32.960
So some.

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:35.912
That's precisely how it
computes an equilibrium point.

00:37:35.912 --> 00:37:38.120
So here, OK, so if you don't
mind, for everyone else,

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:39.370
you probably get some of this.

00:37:39.370 --> 00:37:40.880
But I am going to
go into dialect

00:37:40.880 --> 00:37:42.650
a little bit for a while.

00:37:42.650 --> 00:37:44.570
If you have a circuit,
say I have a circuit

00:37:44.570 --> 00:37:47.060
with a bunch of devices.

00:37:47.060 --> 00:37:48.560
And these are all
non-linear, right?

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:55.100
So all you want to do is solve
a set of 1 billion nonlinear

00:37:55.100 --> 00:37:56.120
equations, right?

00:37:56.120 --> 00:37:59.900
And those equations are things
like KCL, KVL, and the device--

00:37:59.900 --> 00:38:01.880
and that the device--

00:38:01.880 --> 00:38:06.233
and I guess you have to satisfy
the device equations, OK.

00:38:06.233 --> 00:38:08.900
I mean, I think this is fine for
everybody to understand, right?

00:38:08.900 --> 00:38:11.390
So basically, it has to satisfy
physics and the devices.

00:38:11.390 --> 00:38:13.130
The physics is
linear by the way.

00:38:13.130 --> 00:38:15.635
It's just actually
conservation of current

00:38:15.635 --> 00:38:16.760
and all that kind of stuff.

00:38:16.760 --> 00:38:19.460
That's all-- that's fine.

00:38:19.460 --> 00:38:22.320
It's the device equations
that are nonlinear, right?

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:23.370
OK.

00:38:23.370 --> 00:38:25.095
So I don't know.

00:38:25.095 --> 00:38:26.220
Someone who knows circuits.

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:27.810
Do you want to guess a--

00:38:27.810 --> 00:38:29.837
so I give you a circuit.

00:38:29.837 --> 00:38:31.170
It doesn't have to be a billion.

00:38:31.170 --> 00:38:31.890
It's an O pump.

00:38:31.890 --> 00:38:36.600
I got, whatever, 15 device
transistors in it or devices.

00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:39.870
And I'd say, please, find
me the equilibrium or bias--

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:41.040
that's dialect--

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:42.570
bias for the equilibrium point.

00:38:42.570 --> 00:38:44.100
Everybody got this?

00:38:44.100 --> 00:38:45.570
OK.

00:38:45.570 --> 00:38:50.792
Somebody want to-- can someone
guess a homotopy parameter?

00:38:50.792 --> 00:38:53.250
This is just for people who
know about circuits and circuit

00:38:53.250 --> 00:38:55.530
design.

00:38:55.530 --> 00:38:56.800
OK, I'll tell you what it is.

00:38:56.800 --> 00:38:59.140
It's a supply voltage.

00:38:59.140 --> 00:39:03.430
If I take a circuit and I set
the supply voltage to zero,

00:39:03.430 --> 00:39:05.140
your specialty could
be genetics and I

00:39:05.140 --> 00:39:06.470
think you could probably solve.

00:39:06.470 --> 00:39:09.070
So you could say what
the solution is, right?

00:39:09.070 --> 00:39:11.410
In other words, if
I take a circuit

00:39:11.410 --> 00:39:14.710
and I don't apply power to
it, then here's a solution.

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:15.640
Ready?

00:39:15.640 --> 00:39:17.140
All voltages are zero.

00:39:17.140 --> 00:39:18.220
All potentials are zero.

00:39:18.220 --> 00:39:19.137
All currents are zero.

00:39:19.137 --> 00:39:21.012
And that will actually
satisfy the equations.

00:39:21.012 --> 00:39:22.120
Everybody following this?

00:39:22.120 --> 00:39:25.072
So now, I crank up
the supply voltage.

00:39:25.072 --> 00:39:25.780
And again, sorry.

00:39:25.780 --> 00:39:28.280
This is going to be only for
people who know about circuits.

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:30.370
You know the supply
voltage is like 2 volts.

00:39:30.370 --> 00:39:33.370
I set it up to 50
millivolts and I

00:39:33.370 --> 00:39:37.690
attempt to solve again for
an equilibrium point, right.

00:39:37.690 --> 00:39:42.610
So I mean, what is a chip
look like when I put VDD--

00:39:42.610 --> 00:39:44.830
when I put the supply
voltage to 50 millivolts?

00:39:44.830 --> 00:39:46.390
It looks like it's
a resistor, right,

00:39:46.390 --> 00:39:48.430
because you don't have
enough volt-- there's not--

00:39:48.430 --> 00:39:49.870
you don't have enough
voltage to actually turn

00:39:49.870 --> 00:39:50.920
any transistors on.

00:39:50.920 --> 00:39:53.500
Everybody-- I mean, OK,
if you know devices,

00:39:53.500 --> 00:39:55.180
you know what I'm
talking about, right.

00:39:55.180 --> 00:39:56.020
OK.

00:39:56.020 --> 00:39:58.180
Is linear but that
means we can probably--

00:39:58.180 --> 00:40:00.320
we can solve it in one
or two Newton steps.

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:02.570
In fact, the tradition is one.

00:40:02.570 --> 00:40:03.110
OK.

00:40:03.110 --> 00:40:06.920
Then you set it to
100 millivolts, right.

00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:09.860
And you keep going until
you get to whatever 2 volts

00:40:09.860 --> 00:40:11.760
and then you say
that's where I have it.

00:40:11.760 --> 00:40:12.890
Everybody got this?

00:40:12.890 --> 00:40:14.143
So OK.

00:40:14.143 --> 00:40:15.560
So these are
homotopy methods this

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:16.760
is super cool to know about it.

00:40:16.760 --> 00:40:17.090
OK.

00:40:17.090 --> 00:40:18.320
This is a homotopy method.

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:20.420
But there's a big difference.

00:40:20.420 --> 00:40:24.320
Here, I can get to
any point on that path

00:40:24.320 --> 00:40:26.600
from anywhere, which is false.

00:40:26.600 --> 00:40:28.550
In a general
homotopy method, you

00:40:28.550 --> 00:40:31.190
have all this logic about what
happens if you fail, right?

00:40:31.190 --> 00:40:32.730
Oh, let's talk about that.

00:40:32.730 --> 00:40:34.650
Suppose t was 0.05.

00:40:34.650 --> 00:40:36.830
And now I set it to 0.1
and I tried Newton's method

00:40:36.830 --> 00:40:39.575
and it just completely failed,
what would you suggest?

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:46.010
0.075.

00:40:46.010 --> 00:40:47.390
Perfect 0.075.

00:40:47.390 --> 00:40:48.140
I totally agree.

00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:50.300
That's exactly what you do.

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:52.940
It means you were too
aggressive, right,

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:57.020
and you moved too far and
the problem got so different

00:40:57.020 --> 00:41:00.480
that from where you are, you
couldn't get to this thing.

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:00.980
OK.

00:41:00.980 --> 00:41:02.210
So that's exactly
how those were.

00:41:02.210 --> 00:41:04.335
Also, in the general case,
all sorts of sick things

00:41:04.335 --> 00:41:05.030
can happen.

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:09.240
Like they're called-- so two
very bad things can happen,

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:11.150
which is if you're
following a path--

00:41:11.150 --> 00:41:13.950
this isn't the general
case, which is not ours.

00:41:13.950 --> 00:41:15.440
So here's a path.

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:17.240
And one bad thing
that can happen

00:41:17.240 --> 00:41:20.840
is it can so-called bifurcate
and go like this, right?

00:41:20.840 --> 00:41:23.660
So that's one.

00:41:23.660 --> 00:41:27.020
Another one is a path can just
look like this and just stop

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:29.600
and that's it.

00:41:29.600 --> 00:41:32.180
There may be another one
that goes all the way

00:41:32.180 --> 00:41:34.400
to the solution here.

00:41:34.400 --> 00:41:37.160
So these issues
you don't have here

00:41:37.160 --> 00:41:38.420
because we have a single path.

00:41:38.420 --> 00:41:40.293
We can get back to it
from anywhere we like

00:41:40.293 --> 00:41:42.710
and the only question is how
much effort-- how many Newton

00:41:42.710 --> 00:41:43.752
steps it's going to take.

00:41:43.752 --> 00:41:47.160
So I don't know if that made
sense but that is the idea.

00:41:47.160 --> 00:41:49.330
OK.

00:41:49.330 --> 00:41:56.500
So now, you will recognize
this as a homotopy method.

00:41:56.500 --> 00:41:58.420
So here it is.

00:41:58.420 --> 00:42:02.790
It says you pick some t and
you compute x star of t,

00:42:02.790 --> 00:42:06.030
that's Newton's method.

00:42:06.030 --> 00:42:09.420
Then what you do, you quit if
m over t is less than epsilon.

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:10.120
That's fine.

00:42:10.120 --> 00:42:12.210
Otherwise, you increase t.

00:42:12.210 --> 00:42:13.440
This is a very simple method.

00:42:13.440 --> 00:42:15.120
There's much more
sophisticated methods.

00:42:15.120 --> 00:42:16.370
But this is the way you do it.

00:42:16.370 --> 00:42:18.330
You just increase
t by mu, right?

00:42:18.330 --> 00:42:22.830
And so mu is this number
that tells you how much you--

00:42:22.830 --> 00:42:25.830
actually, since the
gap is m over t,

00:42:25.830 --> 00:42:28.770
mu actually is
telling you how much

00:42:28.770 --> 00:42:32.065
are you going to tighten the gap
requirement in the next step.

00:42:32.065 --> 00:42:32.940
That's what it tells.

00:42:32.940 --> 00:42:38.250
So if mu is like 1.05, I
mean, basically, the argument

00:42:38.250 --> 00:42:42.870
would be you just calculated the
analytic center at this point.

00:42:42.870 --> 00:42:48.900
When you increase t by 5%,
it hasn't moved very far.

00:42:48.900 --> 00:42:50.700
Another way to say
it is you're just

00:42:50.700 --> 00:42:53.710
about to solve a problem that's
very similar to the one you

00:42:53.710 --> 00:42:54.850
just solved.

00:42:54.850 --> 00:42:59.450
And in fact, it's going to
take one or two Newton steps.

00:42:59.450 --> 00:42:59.950
OK.

00:42:59.950 --> 00:43:02.650
And in fact, I think, methods,
there are some methods

00:43:02.650 --> 00:43:05.800
that increment t
so in such a way

00:43:05.800 --> 00:43:09.040
that it's so small the
increment that one Newton

00:43:09.040 --> 00:43:10.220
step is all you need.

00:43:10.220 --> 00:43:13.420
So you do one Newton
step and increase t, then

00:43:13.420 --> 00:43:14.840
1 Newton step, and so on.

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:15.880
So that's one way.

00:43:15.880 --> 00:43:18.250
This is called short
step methods, I think.

00:43:18.250 --> 00:43:19.050
OK.

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:19.550
OK.

00:43:19.550 --> 00:43:21.080
Everybody got this?

00:43:21.080 --> 00:43:22.130
OK.

00:43:22.130 --> 00:43:24.720
The luxury we have
is the following.

00:43:24.720 --> 00:43:27.650
This algorithm
cannot fail for us.

00:43:27.650 --> 00:43:30.170
I mean, it might fail for
numerical reasons but in theory

00:43:30.170 --> 00:43:31.520
it, absolutely, it cannot fail.

00:43:31.520 --> 00:43:32.930
It always works.

00:43:32.930 --> 00:43:35.210
So it just does, right?

00:43:35.210 --> 00:43:38.900
Because no matter how
aggressively you update it,

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:41.150
Newton's method is going to
solve this smooth problem.

00:43:41.150 --> 00:43:45.140
It may take a lot of iterations
but it's going to do it.

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:46.530
OK.

00:43:46.530 --> 00:43:47.030
OK.

00:43:47.030 --> 00:43:50.720
So this is the idea.

00:43:50.720 --> 00:43:51.320
OK.

00:43:51.320 --> 00:43:53.570
So it turns out--

00:43:53.570 --> 00:43:55.850
actually, it depends.

00:43:55.850 --> 00:43:58.760
If you talk to the theorists,
mu is going to be really small.

00:43:58.760 --> 00:44:01.550
Like it's going to
literally be like 1 plus

00:44:01.550 --> 00:44:05.180
1 over m or something like that.

00:44:05.180 --> 00:44:08.540
And that's-- I have nothing,
no problem with that.

00:44:08.540 --> 00:44:10.580
It turns out in practice,
people will choose

00:44:10.580 --> 00:44:13.100
things like 10, 20, 50 or 100.

00:44:13.100 --> 00:44:14.450
Something like that.

00:44:14.450 --> 00:44:17.450
Really quite large values.

00:44:17.450 --> 00:44:18.660
OK.

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:19.160
Yeah.

00:44:19.160 --> 00:44:21.560
And there's plenty of
heuristics for choosing

00:44:21.560 --> 00:44:23.240
t0 and stuff like that.

00:44:23.240 --> 00:44:24.200
OK.

00:44:24.200 --> 00:44:26.280
So that's it.

00:44:26.280 --> 00:44:29.660
This is the barrier method.

00:44:29.660 --> 00:44:31.880
OK.

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:34.070
Convergence analysis is
actually kind of interesting

00:44:34.070 --> 00:44:37.070
and it works like this.

00:44:37.070 --> 00:44:43.550
Every time you recompute a
new point on the central path,

00:44:43.550 --> 00:44:46.940
the new gap went down
by a factor of mu.

00:44:46.940 --> 00:44:48.530
Because actually,
that mu is just

00:44:48.530 --> 00:44:52.032
controlling like how much you
tighten your gap each time.

00:44:52.032 --> 00:44:53.490
So what's interesting
about this is

00:44:53.490 --> 00:44:56.300
there's no surprise after
the end of an iteration what

00:44:56.300 --> 00:44:57.440
your gap is.

00:44:57.440 --> 00:45:00.222
Your gap is this provable
distance between how far you

00:45:00.222 --> 00:45:01.430
are and the optimal value is.

00:45:01.430 --> 00:45:03.860
And you can imagine a lot
of algorithms go like this.

00:45:03.860 --> 00:45:05.480
You finish all your calculation.

00:45:05.480 --> 00:45:07.610
Then there's a drum
roll and someone

00:45:07.610 --> 00:45:10.710
you evaluate the dual function,
which is a lower bound.

00:45:10.710 --> 00:45:13.550
And then you look up
and go like, oh, my god.

00:45:13.550 --> 00:45:14.180
That's awesome.

00:45:14.180 --> 00:45:15.230
I'm already 1%--

00:45:15.230 --> 00:45:18.530
I'm provably 1% suboptimal
and I've only done blah, blah,

00:45:18.530 --> 00:45:19.647
blah, 22 iterations.

00:45:19.647 --> 00:45:21.480
Everybody-- so that's
how it usually-- here,

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:22.710
there's no surprise.

00:45:22.710 --> 00:45:25.260
The gap will be,
period, m over t.

00:45:25.260 --> 00:45:27.580
So you basically
dial in the gap.

00:45:27.580 --> 00:45:28.920
That's how that works.

00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:30.780
OK.

00:45:30.780 --> 00:45:34.230
So what that says is that
every time you do a centering,

00:45:34.230 --> 00:45:37.590
you reduce the gap
by a factor mu.

00:45:37.590 --> 00:45:39.840
So that says that the
number of iterations

00:45:39.840 --> 00:45:43.530
is going to take is like an
original gap divided by log mu.

00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:45.650
And so that's going to be the--

00:45:45.650 --> 00:45:49.030
that's the number of
iterations you take.

00:45:49.030 --> 00:45:53.740
Now, the centering--
but then remember,

00:45:53.740 --> 00:45:55.450
you have to do the
centering, right,

00:45:55.450 --> 00:45:57.267
and the real measure
is like let's say

00:45:57.267 --> 00:45:59.350
we could use how many
Newton steps do you actually

00:45:59.350 --> 00:46:01.210
end up performing.

00:46:01.210 --> 00:46:04.930
That would be one
way to do this.

00:46:04.930 --> 00:46:08.700
And if you do the classical
analysis, what you get

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:10.710
is something very weird.

00:46:10.710 --> 00:46:13.840
It goes like-- the classical
analysis says, oh, yeah.

00:46:13.840 --> 00:46:14.340
Yeah.

00:46:14.340 --> 00:46:16.920
I can minimize this for you.

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:17.880
No problem.

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:19.170
I can minimize that.

00:46:19.170 --> 00:46:21.888
And you get a Lipschitz
constant and a strong convexity

00:46:21.888 --> 00:46:23.430
and blah, blah, blah
and you do this.

00:46:23.430 --> 00:46:26.130
But what the classical
analysis will predict

00:46:26.130 --> 00:46:28.320
is that as t gets
bigger and bigger,

00:46:28.320 --> 00:46:31.440
the classical
analysis will tell you

00:46:31.440 --> 00:46:34.480
that the number of
Newton steps will grow.

00:46:34.480 --> 00:46:37.380
And in fact, it'll grow
like alarmingly fast.

00:46:37.380 --> 00:46:38.250
OK?

00:46:38.250 --> 00:46:41.067
And then when you work this out,
you realize it just doesn't--

00:46:41.067 --> 00:46:43.650
I mean, if you're a theorist and
you assemble all these things

00:46:43.650 --> 00:46:46.920
together, you'll find out it's
going to grow really fast,

00:46:46.920 --> 00:46:49.045
right, and it won't
get you what you want,

00:46:49.045 --> 00:46:50.670
which is like a
polynomial time method.

00:46:50.670 --> 00:46:51.660
Yes.

00:46:51.660 --> 00:46:56.070
What happens if you don't like
always solve the minimization

00:46:56.070 --> 00:46:56.790
problem fully?

00:46:56.790 --> 00:46:58.920
Because you don't need
a better point, right?

00:46:58.920 --> 00:46:59.430
Yeah.

00:46:59.430 --> 00:47:01.650
So that's exactly right.

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:05.290
So when you're centering, for
sure, the last two iterations--

00:47:05.290 --> 00:47:08.980
Newton steps were
completely a waste of time.

00:47:08.980 --> 00:47:11.830
And in fact, real
methods don't do that.

00:47:11.830 --> 00:47:12.997
You never solve it directly.

00:47:12.997 --> 00:47:14.913
By the time, as a matter
of fact, what they do

00:47:14.913 --> 00:47:16.480
is by the time
you're in the region

00:47:16.480 --> 00:47:20.080
of quadratic convergence,
t gets updated then.

00:47:20.080 --> 00:47:22.417
So like once you're
there, you're like--

00:47:22.417 --> 00:47:23.500
Well, it's silly because--

00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:25.380
--the first convergence.

00:47:25.380 --> 00:47:27.250
Let's draw that picture, right?

00:47:27.250 --> 00:47:29.740
So that picture looks
like this, right?

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:32.290
You know, here's the
central path, right.

00:47:32.290 --> 00:47:33.280
You're here.

00:47:33.280 --> 00:47:34.400
You're going here.

00:47:34.400 --> 00:47:35.770
Here's your Newton steps.

00:47:35.770 --> 00:47:38.697
First, you go like here, here.

00:47:38.697 --> 00:47:40.280
I'll make it take a
couple more steps.

00:47:40.280 --> 00:47:41.740
Here's what-- it goes like that.

00:47:41.740 --> 00:47:43.660
But then you get close, right.

00:47:43.660 --> 00:47:45.940
And now you do two more
steps that we can't see here

00:47:45.940 --> 00:47:46.970
on the board, right.

00:47:46.970 --> 00:47:49.885
And so these are
a complete waste

00:47:49.885 --> 00:47:52.010
of time if you think about
it because someone says,

00:47:52.010 --> 00:47:52.802
what are you doing?

00:47:52.802 --> 00:47:55.090
You go, well, I'm
going back to the path

00:47:55.090 --> 00:47:57.800
because I want to be on the
path with double precision,

00:47:57.800 --> 00:47:58.640
accuracy.

00:47:58.640 --> 00:48:01.840
And someone's like,
dude, what is the path?

00:48:01.840 --> 00:48:06.050
I asked you to solve like an
SOCP or a quadratic program.

00:48:06.050 --> 00:48:08.658
So what is this path thing
and why should I care?

00:48:08.658 --> 00:48:11.200
And the answer is you shouldn't
care because the path is just

00:48:11.200 --> 00:48:13.090
a construct for us
to figure out a way

00:48:13.090 --> 00:48:16.270
to get to the solution,
which is over here.

00:48:16.270 --> 00:48:18.880
So in fact, all
the analysis work.

00:48:18.880 --> 00:48:21.700
And in fact, if you
have fancy methods,

00:48:21.700 --> 00:48:24.310
they will not do this, right?

00:48:24.310 --> 00:48:27.280
But in this method, you do that
and you're completely right

00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:31.270
the last step or two or three
are just completely wasted.

00:48:31.270 --> 00:48:32.620
So OK.

00:48:32.620 --> 00:48:33.670
There's a question.

00:48:33.670 --> 00:48:36.730
In practice, how much of
a gain does this centering

00:48:36.730 --> 00:48:39.730
give you rather than just
starting with a very large team

00:48:39.730 --> 00:48:41.470
and solving it with that?

00:48:41.470 --> 00:48:45.490
It's usually
infinity in practice.

00:48:45.490 --> 00:48:47.230
In theory, it's not infinity.

00:48:47.230 --> 00:48:49.450
It's just unbelievably large.

00:48:49.450 --> 00:48:51.340
And the reason is
in practice, what

00:48:51.340 --> 00:48:54.310
happens is if I say
solve my LP to six

00:48:54.310 --> 00:48:57.400
significant digits or something,
right, then you got no problem.

00:48:57.400 --> 00:49:02.470
t equals 1 e plus 7 or
something like that.

00:49:02.470 --> 00:49:05.860
Now, in theory, Newton, you
could just do it in one step.

00:49:05.860 --> 00:49:08.560
You just solve that one just
run Newton's method now.

00:49:08.560 --> 00:49:11.200
The problem-- what's going to
happen then is you're going

00:49:11.200 --> 00:49:11.980
to get into--

00:49:11.980 --> 00:49:13.780
you're going to get
into numerical issues

00:49:13.780 --> 00:49:16.425
and it's just not going to work.

00:49:16.425 --> 00:49:17.800
And even if it
did work, it would

00:49:17.800 --> 00:49:19.070
take a gazillion iterations.

00:49:19.070 --> 00:49:19.570
Yeah.

00:49:19.570 --> 00:49:22.130
So in practice, it's
very considerable.

00:49:22.130 --> 00:49:22.630
Yeah.

00:49:22.630 --> 00:49:23.775
So yeah.

00:49:23.775 --> 00:49:25.150
I just want to
make sure that I'm

00:49:25.150 --> 00:49:28.870
understanding this like
offset of m over t correctly.

00:49:28.870 --> 00:49:30.940
So does that mean
that if you only

00:49:30.940 --> 00:49:33.130
cared about what p
star is, you could just

00:49:33.130 --> 00:49:35.110
solve the problem for
one t and just add--

00:49:35.110 --> 00:49:36.070
That's correct.

00:49:36.070 --> 00:49:36.790
Right.

00:49:36.790 --> 00:49:40.030
That's what I call the
UMT method, Unconstrained

00:49:40.030 --> 00:49:41.110
Minimization Technique.

00:49:41.110 --> 00:49:43.210
That's completely correct.

00:49:43.210 --> 00:49:43.710
Yeah.

00:49:43.710 --> 00:49:44.970
That's right.

00:49:44.970 --> 00:49:47.040
What this says--

00:49:47.040 --> 00:49:48.570
I get that question
was what happens

00:49:48.570 --> 00:49:50.460
if you do that but then
someone says, I want

00:49:50.460 --> 00:49:52.530
I want 1e minus 7 accuracy.

00:49:52.530 --> 00:49:54.150
And so they go, no problem.

00:49:54.150 --> 00:49:57.360
t equals 1 e plus 9.

00:49:57.360 --> 00:49:58.830
It will work in theory.

00:49:58.830 --> 00:50:01.440
But I mean, numerically, at
that point, it's not going to.

00:50:01.440 --> 00:50:04.200
I mean that corresponds to the
person who comes over here,

00:50:04.200 --> 00:50:06.060
takes this thing, right.

00:50:06.060 --> 00:50:08.370
I mean, t equals 1
plus 9 corresponds

00:50:08.370 --> 00:50:09.930
to basically the
someone who just

00:50:09.930 --> 00:50:13.200
says give me some sandpaper,
sands off this rough corner

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:14.370
and says, done.

00:50:14.370 --> 00:50:15.360
It's smooth.

00:50:15.360 --> 00:50:17.370
Go ahead and use
your Newton method.

00:50:17.370 --> 00:50:19.020
Everybody following this, right?

00:50:19.020 --> 00:50:23.928
So we know now the theory
is that that will work,

00:50:23.928 --> 00:50:25.470
but it will take a
lot of iterations.

00:50:25.470 --> 00:50:27.720
And the practice is that
it will take, number one,

00:50:27.720 --> 00:50:29.178
it will take a
gazillion iterations

00:50:29.178 --> 00:50:31.020
or most likely just fail.

00:50:31.020 --> 00:50:33.750
So OK.

00:50:33.750 --> 00:50:34.350
OK.

00:50:34.350 --> 00:50:35.910
Everybody got this?

00:50:35.910 --> 00:50:38.850
So the classical analysis
actually tells you

00:50:38.850 --> 00:50:41.100
that as t gets
bigger and bigger,

00:50:41.100 --> 00:50:43.470
it's going to take you
more and more Newton steps

00:50:43.470 --> 00:50:46.155
to get to recenter, right?

00:50:46.155 --> 00:50:48.030
And the reason is pretty
clear because what's

00:50:48.030 --> 00:50:51.240
going to happen is t gets bigger
and bigger, both that Lipschitz

00:50:51.240 --> 00:50:54.300
constant and that strong
convexity constant are

00:50:54.300 --> 00:50:56.850
going to scale up.

00:50:56.850 --> 00:51:00.348
But I mean, that's silly
because that's someone saying

00:51:00.348 --> 00:51:02.640
your algorithm doesn't-- if
someone said your algorithm

00:51:02.640 --> 00:51:03.930
doesn't work, you'd say, why?

00:51:03.930 --> 00:51:06.120
And you'd go, well,
because my analysis

00:51:06.120 --> 00:51:09.210
predicts an upper bound on the
number of steps that's growing.

00:51:09.210 --> 00:51:12.210
And the point is the analysis
turns out to be just wrong.

00:51:12.210 --> 00:51:14.880
So empirically, it's found
that the number of steps

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:17.910
don't grow as t gets
bigger and bigger.

00:51:17.910 --> 00:51:21.370
Again, it's critical that you
start from the previous value.

00:51:21.370 --> 00:51:23.850
So that's the critical part.

00:51:23.850 --> 00:51:27.180
And we'll see that self
concordance actually

00:51:27.180 --> 00:51:29.160
verifies that completely.

00:51:29.160 --> 00:51:31.940
So we'll see that in a minute.

00:51:31.940 --> 00:51:33.590
OK.

00:51:33.590 --> 00:51:35.970
So let's look at some examples.

00:51:35.970 --> 00:51:37.280
So this is just an LP.

00:51:37.280 --> 00:51:40.310
As a matter of fact, you are
going to implement exactly this

00:51:40.310 --> 00:51:42.245
by a week from tomorrow.

00:51:45.030 --> 00:51:47.070
And OK.

00:51:47.070 --> 00:51:49.530
So let's take-- this
is the duality gap.

00:51:49.530 --> 00:51:52.290
That's the gap between
the primal value you found

00:51:52.290 --> 00:51:53.710
and the dual value.

00:51:53.710 --> 00:51:56.220
So this produces both also
not just a primal value

00:51:56.220 --> 00:51:58.500
but dual, of course,
as well, right.

00:51:58.500 --> 00:52:00.210
And so these are
the staircase plots,

00:52:00.210 --> 00:52:02.110
and let me explain
what they are.

00:52:02.110 --> 00:52:05.370
This is Newton's steps.

00:52:05.370 --> 00:52:09.480
The width of the width of
the tread in the staircase

00:52:09.480 --> 00:52:12.510
is the number of Newton
steps per centering.

00:52:12.510 --> 00:52:16.590
The drop is precisely
it's dividing

00:52:16.590 --> 00:52:18.420
the gap by a factor of mu.

00:52:18.420 --> 00:52:21.930
So on a semi-log
plot, those stairs

00:52:21.930 --> 00:52:25.080
have a constant rise,
right, each one.

00:52:25.080 --> 00:52:29.400
And the rise is precisely 1
over mu, right, or something

00:52:29.400 --> 00:52:29.950
like that.

00:52:29.950 --> 00:52:33.600
In other words, you reduce
the gap by a factor of mu.

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:34.800
OK.

00:52:34.800 --> 00:52:37.710
So we can look and we can see
all sorts of cool things here.

00:52:37.710 --> 00:52:41.370
Like, let's take
the mu equals 51.

00:52:41.370 --> 00:52:44.590
mu equals 50 says
you are aggressively

00:52:44.590 --> 00:52:46.970
updating t each step.

00:52:46.970 --> 00:52:48.220
And sure enough, look at that.

00:52:48.220 --> 00:52:51.970
It takes you all of oh,
you know, 11 iterations

00:52:51.970 --> 00:52:53.170
to do the first centering.

00:52:53.170 --> 00:52:55.378
The next one takes
also like 10 or 11.

00:52:55.378 --> 00:52:56.170
Everybody see that?

00:52:56.170 --> 00:52:58.090
That's the width
of those stairs.

00:52:58.090 --> 00:53:01.540
Now, when you do center,
the gap goes down

00:53:01.540 --> 00:53:05.170
by 50x, which is pretty decent.

00:53:05.170 --> 00:53:06.910
Everybody, right?

00:53:06.910 --> 00:53:11.980
You can-- here's little mousy
steps. mu equals 2, right?

00:53:11.980 --> 00:53:15.040
Actually, it'll be hilarious
because the theory will be

00:53:15.040 --> 00:53:17.950
calling for mu equals 1.0001.

00:53:17.950 --> 00:53:19.780
But that's another story.

00:53:19.780 --> 00:53:22.840
But if you look at
mu equals 2, again,

00:53:22.840 --> 00:53:25.300
what you see are the
treads are shorter.

00:53:25.300 --> 00:53:28.030
They're shorter because the
Newton problems you're solving

00:53:28.030 --> 00:53:28.840
are easier.

00:53:28.840 --> 00:53:32.590
Basically, what's
happening is you've

00:53:32.590 --> 00:53:35.390
computed a point like here.

00:53:35.390 --> 00:53:38.080
And when it's 50x, it's
like way over here,

00:53:38.080 --> 00:53:40.150
you're going to actually
have to do some work.

00:53:40.150 --> 00:53:41.733
But when it's two,
it's just over here

00:53:41.733 --> 00:53:44.150
and it's going to be like two,
three Newton steps and game

00:53:44.150 --> 00:53:44.890
over.

00:53:44.890 --> 00:53:45.540
OK.

00:53:45.540 --> 00:53:47.340
Everybody see this?

00:53:47.340 --> 00:53:49.200
So you get that.

00:53:49.200 --> 00:53:51.270
This is mu equals 150, right?

00:53:51.270 --> 00:53:56.520
So this shows you the number
of Newton iterations versus how

00:53:56.520 --> 00:53:57.960
you choose that parameter mu.

00:53:57.960 --> 00:53:59.940
And by the way, if
you made mu bigger,

00:53:59.940 --> 00:54:03.330
it would start to go up again.

00:54:03.330 --> 00:54:05.610
But what you see is something
actually-- so first,

00:54:05.610 --> 00:54:10.250
well, let's talk about
how weird this is.

00:54:10.250 --> 00:54:12.708
Basically, if you kept
going, obviously, this

00:54:12.708 --> 00:54:14.000
would get to 10 to the minus 8.

00:54:14.000 --> 00:54:16.610
But let's take that as
an acceptable amount

00:54:16.610 --> 00:54:19.070
to reduce your gap, right?

00:54:19.070 --> 00:54:23.410
Well, we just reduced our gap
by a factor of 10 to the eighth.

00:54:23.410 --> 00:54:25.150
And we did the whole thing in--

00:54:25.150 --> 00:54:25.820
I don't know.

00:54:25.820 --> 00:54:27.990
Let's look at this one.

00:54:27.990 --> 00:54:30.100
37 Newton steps.

00:54:30.100 --> 00:54:31.150
Everybody.

00:54:31.150 --> 00:54:33.370
So actually, that
takes just some

00:54:33.370 --> 00:54:35.980
thought to sit there and think
about what this means, right?

00:54:35.980 --> 00:54:41.770
So an LP is you have 50
variables, 100 constraints.

00:54:41.770 --> 00:54:44.260
When you picture this,
it's this polyhedron

00:54:44.260 --> 00:54:47.860
in R50 with 100 phases.

00:54:47.860 --> 00:54:51.550
The number of vertices it
has is just astronomical.

00:54:51.550 --> 00:54:53.170
One of those is the solution.

00:54:53.170 --> 00:54:56.410
Everybody following this, right?

00:54:56.410 --> 00:55:00.700
And what this shows is
like extremely embarrassing

00:55:00.700 --> 00:55:05.230
is that in 30 odd Newton
steps, you actually

00:55:05.230 --> 00:55:09.430
get an extremely high
accuracy solution.

00:55:09.430 --> 00:55:12.610
Let me make that clear, right?

00:55:12.610 --> 00:55:15.550
By the way, you get the exact
same plots look like this

00:55:15.550 --> 00:55:17.050
when the dimensions go up.

00:55:17.050 --> 00:55:20.980
They look exactly the same.

00:55:20.980 --> 00:55:22.240
But even here, it's amazing.

00:55:22.240 --> 00:55:23.900
Like R50 is a pretty--
you know what?

00:55:23.900 --> 00:55:24.400
Yeah.

00:55:24.400 --> 00:55:27.760
Let's-- you would get exactly
the same plot if this was

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:30.568
10,000 inequalities
and whatever, 5,000.

00:55:30.568 --> 00:55:31.360
It wouldn't matter.

00:55:31.360 --> 00:55:33.670
It'd be the same.

00:55:33.670 --> 00:55:37.690
But what this says is
actually very, very weird.

00:55:37.690 --> 00:55:40.630
What it says is if
you think about it,

00:55:40.630 --> 00:55:42.110
you actually do 50 Newton steps.

00:55:42.110 --> 00:55:44.140
So what that means is
in solving this LP,

00:55:44.140 --> 00:55:46.210
you start at some
point, literally

00:55:46.210 --> 00:55:47.950
you ask for directions.

00:55:47.950 --> 00:55:50.588
You calculate things involving
these barrier functions

00:55:50.588 --> 00:55:52.630
and things like that and
the equality constraint.

00:55:52.630 --> 00:55:56.470
Actually, you know exactly what
you're-- you solve a linear KKT

00:55:56.470 --> 00:55:58.000
system and you get a direction.

00:55:58.000 --> 00:55:59.120
Everybody got that?

00:55:59.120 --> 00:56:00.970
Then you do a-- then
on that direction,

00:56:00.970 --> 00:56:03.040
you do a line search.

00:56:03.040 --> 00:56:03.880
Then you stop.

00:56:03.880 --> 00:56:05.930
That's iteration one.

00:56:05.930 --> 00:56:07.130
You're the new point.

00:56:07.130 --> 00:56:10.370
You ask for directions
again, which is just query

00:56:10.370 --> 00:56:12.230
the constraints
and the objective

00:56:12.230 --> 00:56:15.020
and you construct some KKT
matrix and get a new direction

00:56:15.020 --> 00:56:15.740
and go again.

00:56:15.740 --> 00:56:17.670
Everybody got this?

00:56:17.670 --> 00:56:23.090
So let's pretend that that
was in 5,000 variables.

00:56:23.090 --> 00:56:25.460
So that's in 5,000
dimensions, you

00:56:25.460 --> 00:56:31.330
basically you stop and ask
for directions 35 times.

00:56:31.330 --> 00:56:33.832
Everybody getting this?

00:56:33.832 --> 00:56:37.140
This is-- I find it
impressive, right?

00:56:37.140 --> 00:56:40.830
Like R 5,000 is a
really big place, right?

00:56:40.830 --> 00:56:42.870
And so the idea
that you actually

00:56:42.870 --> 00:56:46.260
can do this in a mere 30
Newton steps or 30 steps,

00:56:46.260 --> 00:56:49.470
right, I think is
just stunning, right?

00:56:49.470 --> 00:56:52.110
OK.

00:56:52.110 --> 00:56:53.870
Yeah.

00:56:53.870 --> 00:56:56.480
In the plot on the right,
why are there these little

00:56:56.480 --> 00:56:57.290
like jumps?

00:56:57.290 --> 00:56:59.810
For example, at 148 and then 1--

00:56:59.810 --> 00:57:01.340
They're just-- I don't know.

00:57:01.340 --> 00:57:03.950
But they're just
random perturbations

00:57:03.950 --> 00:57:06.590
of-- because you can
accidentally land somewhere

00:57:06.590 --> 00:57:08.780
where instead of taking
some number of Newton steps,

00:57:08.780 --> 00:57:09.860
you take a lot less.

00:57:09.860 --> 00:57:11.580
That's that dumb
luck phenomenon.

00:57:11.580 --> 00:57:12.860
So it could be that.

00:57:12.860 --> 00:57:13.890
I don't know.

00:57:13.890 --> 00:57:14.390
Yeah.

00:57:14.390 --> 00:57:16.400
I mean, this is presumably
on a single instance.

00:57:16.400 --> 00:57:19.186
If we averaged over
a bunch, that would--

00:57:19.186 --> 00:57:24.000
I can imagine that those
bumps would stay there.

00:57:24.000 --> 00:57:24.500
OK.

00:57:24.500 --> 00:57:27.590
This is also
extremely important.

00:57:27.590 --> 00:57:29.870
And what it says is bizarre.

00:57:29.870 --> 00:57:31.580
If you think about
what mu does, mu

00:57:31.580 --> 00:57:33.050
is the aggressiveness
by which you

00:57:33.050 --> 00:57:35.870
update your homotopy parameter.

00:57:35.870 --> 00:57:39.170
Or if you like, it's
a parameter that

00:57:39.170 --> 00:57:43.080
tells you how aggressively each
step you decrease the target

00:57:43.080 --> 00:57:43.580
gap.

00:57:43.580 --> 00:57:46.550
In fact, it is
literally that number.

00:57:46.550 --> 00:57:49.490
So then, there's a trade off.

00:57:49.490 --> 00:57:51.110
We discussed it several times.

00:57:51.110 --> 00:57:53.648
If mu is small,
like 1.05, you're

00:57:53.648 --> 00:57:54.690
taking little baby steps.

00:57:54.690 --> 00:57:58.410
It's going to take one Newton
step or two to recenter, right?

00:57:58.410 --> 00:58:01.260
But you're only making
5% progress each step.

00:58:01.260 --> 00:58:04.490
Or you could take mu equals 10.

00:58:04.490 --> 00:58:06.530
Good news is you're making--

00:58:06.530 --> 00:58:09.890
you're going to divide
your gap by a factor of 10

00:58:09.890 --> 00:58:10.628
when you finish.

00:58:10.628 --> 00:58:12.920
Bad news is it's going to be
harder for-- they're going

00:58:12.920 --> 00:58:14.003
to take more Newton steps.

00:58:14.003 --> 00:58:15.480
Everybody following this?

00:58:15.480 --> 00:58:18.500
So what this tells you-- and
this is confirmed in lots

00:58:18.500 --> 00:58:20.060
and lots and lots
of other cases--

00:58:20.060 --> 00:58:23.180
is these two effects
basically balance each other.

00:58:23.180 --> 00:58:25.283
And that is super good.

00:58:25.283 --> 00:58:27.200
Because the last thing
you want-- what it says

00:58:27.200 --> 00:58:30.350
is the choice of mu doesn't make
any difference at all, I mean,

00:58:30.350 --> 00:58:33.118
as long as it's within
a factor of hundreds

00:58:33.118 --> 00:58:34.160
of some reasonable value.

00:58:34.160 --> 00:58:35.570
Everybody see this?

00:58:35.570 --> 00:58:38.880
So that's very cool.

00:58:38.880 --> 00:58:39.380
OK.

00:58:39.380 --> 00:58:41.675
So this is what that looks like.

00:58:44.300 --> 00:58:49.790
And I find it pretty spooky,
right, that this works.

00:58:49.790 --> 00:58:51.410
I mean, here's a GP.

00:58:51.410 --> 00:58:54.680
And again, these are small ones.

00:58:54.680 --> 00:58:56.600
But nevertheless,
that's pretty cool.

00:58:56.600 --> 00:58:57.920
Here it is.

00:58:57.920 --> 00:59:02.640
And it takes 20 to
whatever, you know.

00:59:02.640 --> 00:59:07.120
Again, it takes like 30
something iterations.

00:59:07.120 --> 00:59:07.620
Yeah.

00:59:07.620 --> 00:59:09.720
By the way, something
like this would be--

00:59:09.720 --> 00:59:12.840
25 years ago-- would just
be like utterly unknown.

00:59:12.840 --> 00:59:16.050
People were still publishing
papers on fancy custom methods

00:59:16.050 --> 00:59:17.280
to solve GPs.

00:59:17.280 --> 00:59:21.060
So this is maybe 30 years ago.

00:59:21.060 --> 00:59:22.800
So still pretty cool.

00:59:22.800 --> 00:59:23.670
But they are all--

00:59:23.670 --> 00:59:27.840
these things all just start
looking the same, right?

00:59:27.840 --> 00:59:30.540
So here's what it looks
like when you just

00:59:30.540 --> 00:59:32.040
take-- this is--
actually, literally

00:59:32.040 --> 00:59:33.420
this is the one you're going
to do for your homework.

00:59:33.420 --> 00:59:34.800
You're going to implement this.

00:59:34.800 --> 00:59:40.710
And here, we have a
bunch of values of m

00:59:40.710 --> 00:59:46.150
and we are going to solve
like 100 different things.

00:59:46.150 --> 00:59:49.170
What's cool is you look-- this
is the problem size, right.

00:59:49.170 --> 00:59:50.555
And this is the number of steps.

00:59:50.555 --> 00:59:51.930
The first thing
you should notice

00:59:51.930 --> 00:59:54.810
is that it ranges
between 20 and 25,

00:59:54.810 --> 00:59:57.360
with the absolute
maximum being 30.

00:59:57.360 --> 01:00:01.750
And by the way, if you
look at the output, when

01:00:01.750 --> 01:00:04.450
you call the solve
method and CVXPY,

01:00:04.450 --> 01:00:05.920
it's going to call some solver.

01:00:05.920 --> 01:00:08.560
But if it's something like
ECOS or something like that,

01:00:08.560 --> 01:00:09.850
it will actually report--

01:00:09.850 --> 01:00:11.933
it'll actually tell you
how many iterations it is.

01:00:11.933 --> 01:00:15.743
And it's typically
between 20 and 50.

01:00:15.743 --> 01:00:17.660
In fact, I don't think
it's ever more than 50.

01:00:17.660 --> 01:00:21.630
But this is kind
of stunning, right?

01:00:21.630 --> 01:00:22.130
This.

01:00:22.130 --> 01:00:22.630
Right?

01:00:22.630 --> 01:00:26.180
And this confused
a lot of people.

01:00:26.180 --> 01:00:29.162
So you know, first people said,
oh, hey, you know it's like--

01:00:29.162 --> 01:00:30.620
I think, it's like
the square root.

01:00:30.620 --> 01:00:31.580
And someone said,
no, I think it grows

01:00:31.580 --> 01:00:33.200
like the log and whatever.

01:00:33.200 --> 01:00:35.000
And then finally,
what has become clear

01:00:35.000 --> 01:00:40.990
as an empirical fact is
that the growth is O of 1.

01:00:40.990 --> 01:00:45.970
In other words, barrier methods
take between 20 and 50 steps.

01:00:45.970 --> 01:00:46.930
Period.

01:00:46.930 --> 01:00:50.350
And that's just what
they do in any dimension.

01:00:50.350 --> 01:00:54.280
And this is-- yeah, it's
been borne out pretty well.

01:00:54.280 --> 01:00:54.790
Yeah.

01:00:54.790 --> 01:00:55.960
So I have a--

01:00:55.960 --> 01:00:58.340
oh, by the way, those
steps are Newton steps.

01:00:58.340 --> 01:01:02.290
So what that means is
you're solving a linear KKT

01:01:02.290 --> 01:01:03.280
system, right?

01:01:03.280 --> 01:01:08.020
So you're solving literally
an h, a transpose A0 linear--

01:01:08.020 --> 01:01:09.400
set of linear equations.

01:01:09.400 --> 01:01:10.540
That's what it is.

01:01:10.540 --> 01:01:12.160
Now, of course, as
the problem gets

01:01:12.160 --> 01:01:16.780
bigger, that numerical step,
that's going to take you

01:01:16.780 --> 01:01:19.360
more time, obviously.

01:01:19.360 --> 01:01:22.000
So I'm not saying that it
takes the same amount of time

01:01:22.000 --> 01:01:24.758
to solve 100 variable problem
as a million variable problem.

01:01:24.758 --> 01:01:26.800
What I am saying is that
the number of iterations

01:01:26.800 --> 01:01:28.570
are the same.

01:01:28.570 --> 01:01:31.330
So I have a friend
in Edinburgh who

01:01:31.330 --> 01:01:37.150
decided to do solve some LP
with a basic barrier method.

01:01:37.150 --> 01:01:39.520
And I asked him like how
many variables we had.

01:01:39.520 --> 01:01:41.500
And he said, well, I
think we have about--

01:01:41.500 --> 01:01:44.860
I think it was something
like 2 billion variables

01:01:44.860 --> 01:01:49.492
and it was like half a
billion constraints, right?

01:01:49.492 --> 01:01:51.200
And I was like, well,
how do you do that?

01:01:51.200 --> 01:01:54.057
And he had some gigantic
computer, supercomputer,

01:01:54.057 --> 01:01:55.390
somewhere to do all this, right?

01:01:55.390 --> 01:01:56.260
I mean, this is a while ago.

01:01:56.260 --> 01:01:58.280
So I'm sure now it wouldn't
be that big a deal.

01:01:58.280 --> 01:01:59.260
But anyway.

01:01:59.260 --> 01:02:03.248
And I was like,
wow, that's awesome.

01:02:03.248 --> 01:02:05.290
So I asked him, how many
iterations does it take?

01:02:05.290 --> 01:02:09.722
And he said, the same as
for real smaller dimensions.

01:02:09.722 --> 01:02:10.430
I was like, what?

01:02:10.430 --> 01:02:12.940
And he like 22, 23, always.

01:02:12.940 --> 01:02:14.050
I was like, oh.

01:02:14.050 --> 01:02:17.020
So by the way, I asked him, how
much does an iteration cost?

01:02:17.020 --> 01:02:19.840
And he's like, each
iteration I think cost--

01:02:19.840 --> 01:02:22.600
it was something
like 25 minutes.

01:02:22.600 --> 01:02:26.050
And I added to the story
that each iteration--

01:02:26.050 --> 01:02:29.740
when each iteration starts,
the lights in Edinburgh dim.

01:02:29.740 --> 01:02:32.020
But that part is
not true, of course.

01:02:32.020 --> 01:02:35.050
But anyway, so
that was the story.

01:02:35.050 --> 01:02:41.273
So this picture continues as
far as anybody knows forever.

01:02:41.273 --> 01:02:42.190
So this is the number.

01:02:42.190 --> 01:02:45.100
It's one of these
weird just magic things

01:02:45.100 --> 01:02:51.440
that practical problems
are solved in O of 1.

01:02:51.440 --> 01:02:52.340
The iterations.

01:02:52.340 --> 01:02:54.140
But the iterations
are going to scale.

01:02:54.140 --> 01:02:55.670
But actually, you know what?

01:02:55.670 --> 01:02:57.560
The implication of
all this is amazing.

01:02:57.560 --> 01:03:00.110
Because it basically
says you do 20--

01:03:00.110 --> 01:03:04.910
you're going to solve
20 odd Newton steps.

01:03:04.910 --> 01:03:08.982
A Newton step is minimizing
a convex quadratic subject

01:03:08.982 --> 01:03:09.940
to equality constraint.

01:03:09.940 --> 01:03:12.200
It is nothing more than that.

01:03:12.200 --> 01:03:16.000
It also says since the number
of it is between 20 and 50,

01:03:16.000 --> 01:03:18.550
like for all problems
across all fields

01:03:18.550 --> 01:03:21.310
and all sizes of problems
and all that kind of stuff,

01:03:21.310 --> 01:03:22.570
that just doesn't change.

01:03:22.570 --> 01:03:24.340
What it tells you is
everything comes down

01:03:24.340 --> 01:03:26.950
to the linear algebra.

01:03:26.950 --> 01:03:31.038
So that's where--
so when you do well,

01:03:31.038 --> 01:03:32.830
it's not because you
have a fancy algorithm

01:03:32.830 --> 01:03:36.380
because they're just they're
basically perfect now.

01:03:36.380 --> 01:03:38.560
They're more sophisticated
than what I'm showing you

01:03:38.560 --> 01:03:40.678
but they're very, very close.

01:03:40.678 --> 01:03:42.720
Actually, all the advances
are in linear algebra.

01:03:42.720 --> 01:03:44.470
So that's what that's about.

01:03:44.470 --> 01:03:45.055
So OK.

01:03:47.520 --> 01:03:48.020
OK.

01:03:48.020 --> 01:03:52.530
So this just shows
you how this works.

01:03:52.530 --> 01:03:53.030
OK.

01:03:53.030 --> 01:03:55.260
So what we're going
to talk about now--

01:03:55.260 --> 01:03:59.000
so anyway, I think this is
just of like amazing stuff.

01:03:59.000 --> 01:04:02.780
Like as I said,
so I can tell you

01:04:02.780 --> 01:04:05.220
a little bit about the
history of some of this.

01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:10.430
So there are precursors of
this in the '60s, including

01:04:10.430 --> 01:04:12.920
all the way up to developing
like codes, algorithms,

01:04:12.920 --> 01:04:16.100
ones extremely similar to
this, right, in the '60s.

01:04:16.100 --> 01:04:20.090
They fell out of fashion or
were ignored or were unknown,

01:04:20.090 --> 01:04:22.100
like for example, Deakin's work.

01:04:22.100 --> 01:04:23.540
Just completely unknown.

01:04:23.540 --> 01:04:28.970
So what happened was there was--
in the late '80s, early '90s,

01:04:28.970 --> 01:04:30.050
it was rediscovered.

01:04:30.050 --> 01:04:31.550
Of course, when it
was rediscovered,

01:04:31.550 --> 01:04:34.700
it was much more
complicated, obviously.

01:04:34.700 --> 01:04:35.930
And so anyway.

01:04:35.930 --> 01:04:37.670
So people were writing papers.

01:04:37.670 --> 01:04:39.890
And it was just
getting very weird.

01:04:39.890 --> 01:04:42.410
And it actually ended up on
the front cover of The New York

01:04:42.410 --> 01:04:44.480
Times, right, because
it was actually--

01:04:44.480 --> 01:04:47.570
this was the method used to
show that linear programs can be

01:04:47.570 --> 01:04:51.900
solved in polynomial time,
right, which had no--

01:04:51.900 --> 01:04:55.500
it had precisely zero
practical consequences

01:04:55.500 --> 01:04:58.680
because at that point,
people who actually solved

01:04:58.680 --> 01:05:03.420
LPs had been solving them for
40 years at that point using

01:05:03.420 --> 01:05:06.130
the simplex method which
worked unbelievably well.

01:05:06.130 --> 01:05:06.630
Thank you.

01:05:06.630 --> 01:05:07.935
So it had no practical.

01:05:07.935 --> 01:05:09.810
But it was very funny
because it was actually

01:05:09.810 --> 01:05:11.477
on the front page of
The New York Times.

01:05:11.477 --> 01:05:15.210
And it hinted-- it said that
vast numbers of scheduling

01:05:15.210 --> 01:05:17.970
problems and allocation
problems can now be solved.

01:05:17.970 --> 01:05:19.650
And it was like just
completely wrong.

01:05:19.650 --> 01:05:20.460
Anyway.

01:05:20.460 --> 01:05:24.360
But it's kind of
cool because to see--

01:05:24.360 --> 01:05:26.165
I don't know--
people I know have

01:05:26.165 --> 01:05:28.290
occurring on the front
cover of The New York Times.

01:05:28.290 --> 01:05:30.580
Anyway, that's it.

01:05:30.580 --> 01:05:31.080
Yeah.

01:05:31.080 --> 01:05:33.060
By the way, later
some 10 years later,

01:05:33.060 --> 01:05:35.040
people went back,
found the history,

01:05:35.040 --> 01:05:36.780
pulled up these
things from the '60s.

01:05:36.780 --> 01:05:42.150
Someone even took some crazy
Fortran code from the 1960s

01:05:42.150 --> 01:05:45.810
untouched, which was
on cards, read it in,

01:05:45.810 --> 01:05:49.320
updated it to whatever it
was at the time-- to f 90

01:05:49.320 --> 01:05:51.240
or something like
that, updated it,

01:05:51.240 --> 01:05:56.070
and it was hilariously
competitive with the best

01:05:56.070 --> 01:05:58.588
solvers at that time,
which by the way,

01:05:58.588 --> 01:06:00.630
it tells you something
about academics and things

01:06:00.630 --> 01:06:03.990
like that, which is
there are fashions

01:06:03.990 --> 01:06:06.450
and there's all sorts of stuff
that works perfectly well

01:06:06.450 --> 01:06:08.610
but people just ignore
it and move on out

01:06:08.610 --> 01:06:10.270
of snobbery or
ignorance or something.

01:06:10.270 --> 01:06:10.770
Anyway.

01:06:10.770 --> 01:06:13.350
So I thought that was very
embarrassing-- that that--

01:06:13.350 --> 01:06:14.700
for the field, right.

01:06:14.700 --> 01:06:15.600
So OK.

01:06:15.600 --> 01:06:18.460
So OK.

01:06:18.460 --> 01:06:18.960
All right.

01:06:18.960 --> 01:06:22.640
This is-- that's it.

01:06:22.640 --> 01:06:23.450
OK.

01:06:23.450 --> 01:06:26.600
So what we're going
to talk about now

01:06:26.600 --> 01:06:30.860
is feasibility and phase I
methods required actually

01:06:30.860 --> 01:06:33.940
strictly feasible point.

01:06:33.940 --> 01:06:36.680
What I will say is that the
methods people actually use

01:06:36.680 --> 01:06:37.490
don't--

01:06:37.490 --> 01:06:41.630
they use something that does
phase I and phase II altogether

01:06:41.630 --> 01:06:43.640
and all that kind of stuff.

01:06:43.640 --> 01:06:45.913
By the way, if we had one
more week of this class

01:06:45.913 --> 01:06:47.330
you would actually
implement that.

01:06:47.330 --> 01:06:48.602
Of course, it would be about--

01:06:48.602 --> 01:06:50.810
I don't know-- five more
lines than what you're going

01:06:50.810 --> 01:06:51.680
to write--

01:06:51.680 --> 01:06:53.130
and that one would
actually scale

01:06:53.130 --> 01:06:55.130
to handle feasibility,
unboundedness, everything

01:06:55.130 --> 01:06:56.840
just all at once.

01:06:56.840 --> 01:06:58.700
But nevertheless, this
is actually very good

01:06:58.700 --> 01:07:01.250
to know about because it's
got uses, a lot of uses,

01:07:01.250 --> 01:07:03.180
just by itself.

01:07:03.180 --> 01:07:04.880
So here it is.

01:07:04.880 --> 01:07:09.320
So the feasibility
problem is find

01:07:09.320 --> 01:07:11.360
an x that satisfies a
bunch of inequalities

01:07:11.360 --> 01:07:14.520
and maybe some linear
equality constraints, right?

01:07:14.520 --> 01:07:17.190
And so the classical
method, they

01:07:17.190 --> 01:07:19.510
call it a phase I
and phase II method.

01:07:19.510 --> 01:07:22.680
So phase I, find
a feasible point.

01:07:22.680 --> 01:07:27.818
Phase II, run an algorithm
to find an optimal point.

01:07:27.818 --> 01:07:29.110
And this is kind of old school.

01:07:29.110 --> 01:07:34.140
But it's not-- you might someday
encounter people who were

01:07:34.140 --> 01:07:35.520
trained in old school stuff.

01:07:35.520 --> 01:07:37.265
And then if you drop--

01:07:37.265 --> 01:07:39.390
I mean, if you say it
around, a lot of people today

01:07:39.390 --> 01:07:41.307
if you say phase I and
phase II, they're like,

01:07:41.307 --> 01:07:42.810
what are you talking about?

01:07:42.810 --> 01:07:46.570
But it's possible you'll meet
some of these people sometime.

01:07:46.570 --> 01:07:48.100
And the ideas are
very interesting.

01:07:48.100 --> 01:07:48.600
OK.

01:07:48.600 --> 01:07:51.730
So here's what you do.

01:07:51.730 --> 01:07:58.950
What we do is we put
here a slack variable s.

01:07:58.950 --> 01:08:02.220
And we want to minimize
over x and ns this s.

01:08:02.220 --> 01:08:05.280
Now, what we do is we
stop, we terminate this.

01:08:05.280 --> 01:08:09.630
The minute we produce something
with s negative, we quit.

01:08:09.630 --> 01:08:12.900
Because at that point,
what you've done

01:08:12.900 --> 01:08:15.015
is you've computed a
strictly feasible point

01:08:15.015 --> 01:08:16.890
and then you jump back
to the barrier method.

01:08:16.890 --> 01:08:20.700
Or actually, you can apply
the barrier method to this.

01:08:20.700 --> 01:08:22.529
Because here, I know
a feasible point.

01:08:22.529 --> 01:08:24.779
It's like you pick
your favorite point

01:08:24.779 --> 01:08:26.609
x that satisfies Ax equals b.

01:08:26.609 --> 01:08:30.359
OK, it has to be in the domain
of the fI's but whatever.

01:08:30.359 --> 01:08:33.359
What I do is I evaluate
fI of x, all of them,

01:08:33.359 --> 01:08:36.840
I take the max of that, those
numbers, plus 1 and that's

01:08:36.840 --> 01:08:37.529
my initial s.

01:08:37.529 --> 01:08:40.399
It's feasible.

01:08:40.399 --> 01:08:42.630
And so this just--
this would just work.

01:08:42.630 --> 01:08:43.130
OK.

01:08:43.130 --> 01:08:45.529
So that's that.

01:08:45.529 --> 01:08:48.810
But let me ask a
couple of questions.

01:08:48.810 --> 01:08:50.810
So let's ask-- let
me ask what happens

01:08:50.810 --> 01:08:56.460
if these inequalities
are not feasible,

01:08:56.460 --> 01:08:59.660
then what's going to happen is
when you solve this problem,

01:08:59.660 --> 01:09:02.240
you're going to get
s equals like 1.3.

01:09:02.240 --> 01:09:05.120
And that has a beautiful
interpretation.

01:09:05.120 --> 01:09:08.149
It basically says your
inequalities are not

01:09:08.149 --> 01:09:12.910
mutually feasible because
they're all fI less than 0.

01:09:12.910 --> 01:09:15.189
But if you relax those
and everyone looks

01:09:15.189 --> 01:09:19.240
like fI of x is less than
1.3, I got a point for you.

01:09:19.240 --> 01:09:20.600
Everybody following this?

01:09:20.600 --> 01:09:22.450
And so you're kind
of-- basically,

01:09:22.450 --> 01:09:25.000
you can interpret this
basic phase one method

01:09:25.000 --> 01:09:28.990
as minimizing the maximum
constraint violation.

01:09:28.990 --> 01:09:31.689
And now I'm going to
ask you a question.

01:09:31.689 --> 01:09:35.380
Suppose a set of
inequalities is not feasible.

01:09:35.380 --> 01:09:36.910
And here's what I want to know.

01:09:36.910 --> 01:09:38.500
I run this.

01:09:38.500 --> 01:09:41.422
s star turns out to
be 1.3, let's say.

01:09:41.422 --> 01:09:42.880
I want you to tell
me about what do

01:09:42.880 --> 01:09:44.305
you think the fI's look like.

01:09:50.843 --> 01:09:52.010
This is just a guess, right?

01:09:52.010 --> 01:09:53.750
I mean, what do you imagine?

01:09:56.440 --> 01:09:58.750
Quite a lot. m will
be at that area.

01:09:58.750 --> 01:10:00.490
Exactly right.

01:10:00.490 --> 01:10:03.640
Basically, I mean you kind of--
if you're anthropomorphizing

01:10:03.640 --> 01:10:07.060
this, right, you have
all these functions here,

01:10:07.060 --> 01:10:08.920
you want them all
to be less than 0.

01:10:08.920 --> 01:10:11.620
So what you do is you just put
a big old flat thing on top

01:10:11.620 --> 01:10:14.230
of it, that's s, and
you just push down.

01:10:14.230 --> 01:10:17.530
You push down until
it locks and it's

01:10:17.530 --> 01:10:19.780
pushing back harder than
you can push, right?

01:10:19.780 --> 01:10:21.040
In fact, with infinite thing.

01:10:21.040 --> 01:10:23.207
So you can actually make a
mechanical interpretation

01:10:23.207 --> 01:10:24.370
if you wanted.

01:10:24.370 --> 01:10:25.300
Everybody see this?

01:10:25.300 --> 01:10:29.380
Now, when this thing locks
and you can't push it down

01:10:29.380 --> 01:10:32.560
any farther, there's going
to be at least several

01:10:32.560 --> 01:10:36.550
and in general, a whole bunch
of constraints violated by 1.3.

01:10:36.550 --> 01:10:38.320
Everybody got that?

01:10:38.320 --> 01:10:40.630
And there are
applications where that

01:10:40.630 --> 01:10:42.760
might be of interest, right?

01:10:42.760 --> 01:10:44.770
You'd say, I have a whole
bunch of constraints.

01:10:44.770 --> 01:10:47.900
And you'd say, well, I got
good news and bad news.

01:10:47.900 --> 01:10:50.240
The bad news is no
one-- there is no x that

01:10:50.240 --> 01:10:51.490
satisfies all your constraint.

01:10:51.490 --> 01:10:54.130
There's no design that
meets all your specs, right?

01:10:54.130 --> 01:10:58.970
Good news is if I only relax
all of them by like 0.3,

01:10:58.970 --> 01:11:00.080
then it's feasible.

01:11:00.080 --> 01:11:02.760
Everybody see this?

01:11:02.760 --> 01:11:03.260
OK.

01:11:03.260 --> 01:11:05.285
So that's the idea there.

01:11:08.230 --> 01:11:10.020
So if you do--

01:11:10.020 --> 01:11:11.730
then we go to the
next one, which is

01:11:11.730 --> 01:11:13.810
a sum of infeasibility method.

01:11:13.810 --> 01:11:16.260
And so the previous
one you should

01:11:16.260 --> 01:11:20.590
think of as kind of roughly
analogous to an L infinity

01:11:20.590 --> 01:11:21.090
norm.

01:11:21.090 --> 01:11:25.910
We were minimizing the
maximum constraint violation.

01:11:25.910 --> 01:11:29.540
Now, I'm going to minimize
the sum of the constraint

01:11:29.540 --> 01:11:30.230
violation.

01:11:30.230 --> 01:11:35.330
The constraint violation is
actually defined as this.

01:11:35.330 --> 01:11:40.010
It's fI of x sub plus.

01:11:40.010 --> 01:11:44.160
If that's zero, it means
you satisfy that thing.

01:11:44.160 --> 01:11:46.040
Otherwise, this is the
amount by which you

01:11:46.040 --> 01:11:48.080
have violated the constraint.

01:11:48.080 --> 01:11:51.080
And so what this says is
instead-- what we originally

01:11:51.080 --> 01:11:52.820
did was we did this.

01:11:52.820 --> 01:11:56.420
We minimized the
max over I of these.

01:11:56.420 --> 01:11:59.090
I mean, this didn't matter.

01:11:59.090 --> 01:12:00.810
Now, we're going to
do the following.

01:12:00.810 --> 01:12:03.290
We're going to do the sum.

01:12:03.290 --> 01:12:06.380
And before we even do
this, I want to know--

01:12:06.380 --> 01:12:09.500
I want some guesses
about various things.

01:12:09.500 --> 01:12:10.040
Oh.

01:12:10.040 --> 01:12:11.945
These are non-negative numbers.

01:12:14.960 --> 01:12:20.090
A sum of non-negative numbers
is basically the L1 norm, right?

01:12:20.090 --> 01:12:23.600
I mean, this is obvious.

01:12:23.600 --> 01:12:29.160
And when I minimize an L1
norm, what typically happens?

01:12:29.160 --> 01:12:31.610
[INAUDIBLE]

01:12:31.610 --> 01:12:33.470
You're going to get sparsity.

01:12:33.470 --> 01:12:35.750
And so this actually
starts getting

01:12:35.750 --> 01:12:36.860
super interesting, right?

01:12:36.860 --> 01:12:40.670
Because what it is you say,
I'm trying to design this thing

01:12:40.670 --> 01:12:42.560
and you gave me 50 specs.

01:12:42.560 --> 01:12:45.237
And you go, bad
news, can't be done.

01:12:45.237 --> 01:12:46.070
It's not achievable.

01:12:46.070 --> 01:12:47.450
And I can prove it,
by the way, right,

01:12:47.450 --> 01:12:49.010
because you can
produce a certificate

01:12:49.010 --> 01:12:51.290
or a theorem of the
alternatives, a certificate

01:12:51.290 --> 01:12:52.340
showing it.

01:12:52.340 --> 01:12:54.320
And you'd say-- and
they'd say, yeah,

01:12:54.320 --> 01:12:55.670
but I've got to do--
we got to do something.

01:12:55.670 --> 01:12:57.420
We got to build this
product or something.

01:12:57.420 --> 01:12:58.950
And you'd say, that's fine.

01:12:58.950 --> 01:13:01.310
Here's what I did.

01:13:01.310 --> 01:13:02.810
I did find something
that satisfies

01:13:02.810 --> 01:13:06.470
47 of your 50 constraints.

01:13:06.470 --> 01:13:07.850
Everybody got that?

01:13:07.850 --> 01:13:10.820
And that's what you would see
here, which is really cool.

01:13:10.820 --> 01:13:12.440
And here's a picture.

01:13:12.440 --> 01:13:13.938
We can take a look at this.

01:13:13.938 --> 01:13:14.855
Oh, here's an example.

01:13:18.110 --> 01:13:20.900
This is actually the
margin in your constraint.

01:13:20.900 --> 01:13:23.750
So you want to push
this to be above zero.

01:13:23.750 --> 01:13:24.613
This failed.

01:13:24.613 --> 01:13:25.280
It's infeasible.

01:13:25.280 --> 01:13:26.070
You stop there.

01:13:26.070 --> 01:13:28.280
And just as we guessed,
a whole bunch of them

01:13:28.280 --> 01:13:30.583
are lined up at this worst
point, which is minus 0.2

01:13:30.583 --> 01:13:31.250
or I don't know.

01:13:31.250 --> 01:13:31.875
Whatever it is.

01:13:31.875 --> 01:13:33.500
0.2, right?

01:13:33.500 --> 01:13:34.900
OK.

01:13:34.900 --> 01:13:37.520
This is what happens when you
do a sum of infeasibility.

01:13:37.520 --> 01:13:39.560
And it's really cool.

01:13:39.560 --> 01:13:40.895
What it says is--

01:13:40.895 --> 01:13:45.890
I don't know-- 55 of
your fI's are tight.

01:13:45.890 --> 01:13:49.160
Actually, these are all ones.

01:13:49.160 --> 01:13:51.650
They are just-- they
have their slack, right?

01:13:51.650 --> 01:13:54.920
There's-- your whatever fI of x
is actually negative for these.

01:13:54.920 --> 01:13:58.970
And then you have a handful
here, which you can't satisfy.

01:13:58.970 --> 01:14:01.610
And so again, this is
just our old friend,

01:14:01.610 --> 01:14:04.280
the L1 specifier here.

01:14:04.280 --> 01:14:06.250
It's kind of cool,
right, this thing.

01:14:06.250 --> 01:14:09.300
And these are all already
like quite useful right

01:14:09.300 --> 01:14:13.680
in various applications
that I've seen.

01:14:13.680 --> 01:14:14.580
So OK.

01:14:14.580 --> 01:14:16.155
Does this make sense?

01:14:20.970 --> 01:14:24.240
So here's an example.

01:14:24.240 --> 01:14:27.960
So you have just a family
of linear inequalities.

01:14:27.960 --> 01:14:28.850
So it's a polyhedron.

01:14:28.850 --> 01:14:31.580
But I have a delta--

01:14:31.580 --> 01:14:33.620
a gamma-- sorry--
a parameter gamma.

01:14:33.620 --> 01:14:38.210
And it's chosen so that
when gamma equals zero,

01:14:38.210 --> 01:14:41.810
the polyhedron collapses
and has zero volume.

01:14:41.810 --> 01:14:47.840
When gamma is negative or
less than or equal to 0,

01:14:47.840 --> 01:14:48.650
well, it's not--

01:14:48.650 --> 01:14:50.277
when it's negative,
it's infeasible.

01:14:50.277 --> 01:14:51.860
And when it's positive,
it's feasible.

01:14:51.860 --> 01:14:53.902
And that you're just
scaling the right hand side.

01:14:53.902 --> 01:14:57.500
So the polyhedron
is growing, right.

01:14:57.500 --> 01:15:00.440
And now, what we can do is we
can use the basic phase one

01:15:00.440 --> 01:15:03.050
and stuff like that and
look at the number of steps

01:15:03.050 --> 01:15:10.212
it takes to either find a point
right in the first case or--

01:15:10.212 --> 01:15:11.420
so here's what it looks like.

01:15:11.420 --> 01:15:13.620
It's actually super interesting.

01:15:13.620 --> 01:15:16.850
So it looks it looks like
it takes like 40 steps

01:15:16.850 --> 01:15:20.390
unless you're right at
the boundary of between

01:15:20.390 --> 01:15:21.540
feasible and infeasible.

01:15:21.540 --> 01:15:23.280
And that's the
only-- in fact, you

01:15:23.280 --> 01:15:25.440
have to adjust the
data very carefully

01:15:25.440 --> 01:15:30.240
to get more than whatever
30, 40 steps to do this,

01:15:30.240 --> 01:15:32.250
to solve this phase I problem.

01:15:32.250 --> 01:15:33.030
But you can do it.

01:15:33.030 --> 01:15:34.123
I mean, but it's--

01:15:34.123 --> 01:15:36.540
what it says is in practice
it just doesn't happen, right?

01:15:36.540 --> 01:15:38.790
Oh, this is showing you
the zoomed in version.

01:15:38.790 --> 01:15:40.748
Actually, these are sort
of interesting, right,

01:15:40.748 --> 01:15:45.760
because over on the
left, you would say,

01:15:45.760 --> 01:15:47.380
this is taking only 30 steps.

01:15:47.380 --> 01:15:48.100
You'd say, why?

01:15:48.100 --> 01:15:51.310
Because it's
infeasible but there's

01:15:51.310 --> 01:15:52.480
a lot of certificates of--

01:15:52.480 --> 01:15:54.260
it's obviously infeasible.

01:15:54.260 --> 01:15:54.760
It's easy.

01:15:54.760 --> 01:15:58.120
So it doesn't take many
steps to prove infeasibility.

01:15:58.120 --> 01:16:00.460
Like the polyhedron
over here on the right,

01:16:00.460 --> 01:16:02.710
the polyhedron is so big
it doesn't take very long

01:16:02.710 --> 01:16:04.780
to find a feasible point.

01:16:04.780 --> 01:16:07.840
And then the only
time this increases

01:16:07.840 --> 01:16:11.590
is when you get right between
the boundary of what's feasible

01:16:11.590 --> 01:16:12.850
and not feasible, right?

01:16:12.850 --> 01:16:16.217
So that's where it
goes up like that.

01:16:16.217 --> 01:16:17.300
That's what it looks like.

01:16:17.300 --> 01:16:18.850
So OK.

01:16:18.850 --> 01:16:23.140
So that's a picture of that.

01:16:23.140 --> 01:16:25.690
What we'll do is we'll just
start talking about this

01:16:25.690 --> 01:16:29.990
and we'll finish
this up next week.

01:16:29.990 --> 01:16:34.450
But it's actually
really quite cool.

01:16:34.450 --> 01:16:38.140
So we're going to assume
that f0 is going to be

01:16:38.140 --> 01:16:40.980
in the fI's are going to be--

01:16:40.980 --> 01:16:42.730
well, actually, it's
actually the barrier.

01:16:42.730 --> 01:16:47.800
I want tf0 plus phi to be
self-concordant, right?

01:16:47.800 --> 01:16:51.040
So that's the requirement.

01:16:51.040 --> 01:16:54.100
And there might be some--
here's a minimum-- here's

01:16:54.100 --> 01:16:58.000
a maximum entropy problem
with inequality constraints.

01:16:58.000 --> 01:16:59.842
And it turns out that's
not self-concordant.

01:16:59.842 --> 01:17:01.300
Again, you don't
have to know this.

01:17:01.300 --> 01:17:03.592
But when you put in the x
bigger than or equal to zero,

01:17:03.592 --> 01:17:05.422
you add a log barrier
then it becomes--

01:17:05.422 --> 01:17:07.630
that's a redundant constraint
that x bigger and equal

01:17:07.630 --> 01:17:08.530
to zero.

01:17:08.530 --> 01:17:11.320
That's completely redundant
because this objective,

01:17:11.320 --> 01:17:14.590
its domain is x has to be
bigger than or equal to 0.

01:17:14.590 --> 01:17:17.050
So this is completely redundant.

01:17:17.050 --> 01:17:19.220
But when you add that
redundant constraint

01:17:19.220 --> 01:17:20.960
and you work out the
log barrier, which

01:17:20.960 --> 01:17:25.970
is just negative sum log
x I, then that function

01:17:25.970 --> 01:17:28.933
becomes self-concordant.

01:17:28.933 --> 01:17:31.100
And what I should do is I
should preface all of this

01:17:31.100 --> 01:17:33.830
by saying what you're
going to see is super cool

01:17:33.830 --> 01:17:38.660
but it is not at all clear what
its practical implications are.

01:17:38.660 --> 01:17:41.750
That is not clear at all.

01:17:41.750 --> 01:17:44.660
Its theoretical
implications are very clear.

01:17:44.660 --> 01:17:46.940
Practical ones not so much.

01:17:46.940 --> 01:17:50.860
And it's very weird most--

01:17:50.860 --> 01:17:54.340
so actually, it'll change
every couple of years, right?

01:17:54.340 --> 01:17:57.880
People will implement
solvers which are actually

01:17:57.880 --> 01:18:00.580
used self-concordant barriers
and all that kind of stuff

01:18:00.580 --> 01:18:02.980
and actually agree
with the theory.

01:18:02.980 --> 01:18:06.010
But then, they'll find something
that just makes it a lot faster

01:18:06.010 --> 01:18:08.380
and they're like, we don't care.

01:18:08.380 --> 01:18:10.502
Then the theorists will
catch up a few years later

01:18:10.502 --> 01:18:11.710
and write something about it.

01:18:11.710 --> 01:18:13.540
Anyway, so there's just
this oscillation where

01:18:13.540 --> 01:18:14.650
it kind of goes like that.

01:18:14.650 --> 01:18:17.320
But it's not at
all clear you know.

01:18:17.320 --> 01:18:20.125
But I have met weird people
who said things like--

01:18:20.125 --> 01:18:22.000
they said, you know what
barrier you're using

01:18:22.000 --> 01:18:22.750
and you'd write it down.

01:18:22.750 --> 01:18:24.375
And someone would
say like, oh, my god.

01:18:24.375 --> 01:18:26.680
That's not self-concordant.

01:18:26.680 --> 01:18:31.057
You're like, I mean,
the algorithm complexity

01:18:31.057 --> 01:18:32.140
police aren't here, right?

01:18:32.140 --> 01:18:33.918
So is there an
algorithm complexity

01:18:33.918 --> 01:18:36.460
warrant for your arrest or what
are you worried about, right?

01:18:36.460 --> 01:18:38.410
Anyway.

01:18:38.410 --> 01:18:42.640
But nevertheless, it's
very cool, the analysis.

01:18:42.640 --> 01:18:44.390
OK.

01:18:44.390 --> 01:18:44.890
OK.

01:18:44.890 --> 01:18:47.050
So let's take a look at it.

01:18:47.050 --> 01:18:51.700
It's actually just
completely insane.

01:18:51.700 --> 01:18:54.130
It goes like this, right?

01:18:54.130 --> 01:18:58.180
So remember what it is
is there's two terms.

01:18:58.180 --> 01:19:01.540
There's basically the
number of centering steps.

01:19:01.540 --> 01:19:04.610
But that's just log 1 over mu.

01:19:04.610 --> 01:19:06.670
Like that's what it
has to do with, right?

01:19:06.670 --> 01:19:11.200
Mu is this is how
aggressively you walk along

01:19:11.200 --> 01:19:12.490
this central path, right?

01:19:12.490 --> 01:19:14.050
So that's your-- in
fact, people call

01:19:14.050 --> 01:19:15.910
that the homotopy parameter.

01:19:15.910 --> 01:19:19.910
It's how fast you increase
your homotopy parameter.

01:19:19.910 --> 01:19:20.410
OK.

01:19:20.410 --> 01:19:23.320
So the question is, how
many Newton steps does it

01:19:23.320 --> 01:19:25.840
take-- if I'm on
the central path,

01:19:25.840 --> 01:19:29.170
I increase t by a factor
mu, and I have to calculate

01:19:29.170 --> 01:19:30.400
the new point, right?

01:19:30.400 --> 01:19:35.260
That is the-- once we know what
that-- if we bound that number,

01:19:35.260 --> 01:19:37.312
that's the number
of Newton steps--

01:19:37.312 --> 01:19:38.770
that's the maximum
number of Newton

01:19:38.770 --> 01:19:40.870
steps you can take in one
of these centering steps

01:19:40.870 --> 01:19:43.178
and you multiply that by
basically log 1 over mu

01:19:43.178 --> 01:19:45.220
and you get your total
number-- your total count.

01:19:45.220 --> 01:19:46.520
Everybody got this?

01:19:46.520 --> 01:19:47.020
OK.

01:19:47.020 --> 01:19:48.790
So if you remember
what that looked like,

01:19:48.790 --> 01:19:49.760
it looked like this.

01:19:49.760 --> 01:19:52.100
And this sounded kind of weird.

01:19:52.100 --> 01:19:53.750
Basically, the
number of steps looks

01:19:53.750 --> 01:19:57.350
like something like
f of, let's say, x0

01:19:57.350 --> 01:20:04.178
minus then f of x star like
this divided by some number.

01:20:04.178 --> 01:20:05.720
Did I write it out
here or something?

01:20:05.720 --> 01:20:06.220
Yeah.

01:20:06.220 --> 01:20:07.430
I called it gamma there.

01:20:07.430 --> 01:20:09.170
And then plus a constant, right?

01:20:09.170 --> 01:20:10.340
It's not really a constant.

01:20:10.340 --> 01:20:13.320
The constant is really like
a log log something or other.

01:20:13.320 --> 01:20:15.230
But basically, it's fine.

01:20:15.230 --> 01:20:19.008
It's five or something
or-- not even five, right?

01:20:19.008 --> 01:20:20.550
So I don't know if
you remember this.

01:20:20.550 --> 01:20:22.700
But this is the--

01:20:22.700 --> 01:20:24.410
if a function is
self-concordant,

01:20:24.410 --> 01:20:25.880
this is the number--
actually, this

01:20:25.880 --> 01:20:27.380
is the same-- the
classical analysis

01:20:27.380 --> 01:20:28.500
gives you the same thing.

01:20:28.500 --> 01:20:31.490
It's just that c and gamma
depend on all sorts of things

01:20:31.490 --> 01:20:35.010
that you would never know and
are not affinely independent.

01:20:35.010 --> 01:20:36.830
But if f is
self-concordant, we actually

01:20:36.830 --> 01:20:40.430
know what this-- we know
numbers that are valid for this.

01:20:40.430 --> 01:20:41.750
OK.

01:20:41.750 --> 01:20:44.260
Now, there is this
embarrassing thing

01:20:44.260 --> 01:20:46.760
which is someone says, what's
the maximum number of Newton's

01:20:46.760 --> 01:20:47.802
steps it's going to take?

01:20:47.802 --> 01:20:49.340
And you go, well, it's this.

01:20:49.340 --> 01:20:53.180
And you go, yeah, but
how do know this, right?

01:20:53.180 --> 01:20:54.425
And so you get--

01:20:54.425 --> 01:20:56.550
it's very embarrassing
because you'd say, oh, yeah,

01:20:56.550 --> 01:20:57.290
no problem.

01:20:57.290 --> 01:20:59.582
To bound the number of Newton
steps, all you have to do

01:20:59.582 --> 01:21:01.545
is first solve it
using Newton's method.

01:21:01.545 --> 01:21:05.290
Then you know this,
then you subtract this

01:21:05.290 --> 01:21:06.670
and divide and add that.

01:21:06.670 --> 01:21:09.635
And you're like, dude,
I just solved it.

01:21:09.635 --> 01:21:11.260
I know the exact
number of Newton steps

01:21:11.260 --> 01:21:13.180
now so I don't need your
stupid bound anymore.

01:21:13.180 --> 01:21:15.100
Is everyone following
this, right?

01:21:15.100 --> 01:21:17.950
So I mean it's
reasonable, right?

01:21:17.950 --> 01:21:21.640
But we're going
to-- you can even

01:21:21.640 --> 01:21:24.040
imagine what's going
to help us here

01:21:24.040 --> 01:21:29.380
because this bound is a bound if
you had a lower bound on that.

01:21:29.380 --> 01:21:32.130
So do you think we
have a lower bound?

01:21:32.130 --> 01:21:35.070
Where do you imagine a lower
bound on the optimal value

01:21:35.070 --> 01:21:37.960
something is going to come from
before you've actually solved

01:21:37.960 --> 01:21:38.460
it.

01:21:38.460 --> 01:21:41.520
So should we say it all
in unison or whatever?

01:21:41.520 --> 01:21:43.020
But you get one guess.

01:21:43.020 --> 01:21:44.840
Go ahead.

01:21:44.840 --> 01:21:46.420
Duality.

01:21:46.420 --> 01:21:46.920
OK.

01:21:46.920 --> 01:21:49.020
So that's the
highlight of the story.

01:21:49.020 --> 01:21:52.580
